window.KillerBenchSearch=[{"u":"help.html","a":"welcome","g":"Help","t":"Welcome","h":[["welcome","Welcome"],["welcome_what-it-does","What it does"],["welcome_where-to-start","Where to start"]],"x":"KillerBench help KillerBench is a Windows diagnostic and repair workbench for technicians. It checks a computer first, explains what it found with the evidence behind each finding, and only changes Windows when you pick a specific fix and confirm it. KillerBench is in development. There is no public download yet. These pages describe the current build. What it does - Runs read-only diagnostics across Windows Update, devices, storage, networking, printing, security, apps and profiles, then writes a readable report. - Offers guarded repairs that run only when fresh evidence proves the exact problem, and verifies the result afterwards. - Covers setup and hardening work such as BitLocker, privacy settings, decluttering Windows and onboarding a new computer. - Runs diagnostics and report analysis locally without AI or a KillerBench account. Selected actions, such as downloads and recovery-key backup to Entra ID, can contact external services. - Uses the active app theme for the console, reports, confirmations, and Open, Save, and Folder dialogs. Where to start - Download and setup - About KillerBench - Your first diagnosis walks through a full system scan. - The workspace explains the sections, panels and shortcuts. - How changes stay safe explains what KillerBench checks before and after any change. - How-to guides walk through common jobs step by step. - The Sections pages describe every tool, grouped the same way as the app. - Command line lists every killerbench-cli command. - Troubleshooting covers blocked actions, restarts and interrupted requests. Developers who want to embed the diagnostics in their own tools can read the Engine developer guide ."},{"u":"help.html","a":"setup","g":"Help","t":"Download and setup","h":[["setup","Download and setup"],["setup_windows-and-runtime","Windows and runtime"],["setup_when-a-package-is-published","When a package is published"],["setup_current-limits","Current limits"],["setup_source-license-and-support","Source, license and support"]],"x":"Download and setup KillerBench 1.0.0 is Unreleased. No public KillerBench package or download is available yet. This page describes the current package requirements so you can prepare without downloading an unofficial copy. Windows and runtime - The current publish target is Windows x64 . Windows 11 x64 is the current native validation baseline. Native ARM64, x86 and Windows Server distributions are not provided. - KillerBench is packaged two ways. The installer is framework-dependent and requires .NET Desktop Runtime 10 for Windows x64 . The plain .NET Runtime, ASP.NET Core Runtime and .NET Framework do not supply WPF. The portable edition carries its own runtime and needs no separate install. The command-line tool killerbench-cli.exe needs the .NET 10 Runtime for Windows x64. An SDK is needed for development, not for ordinary use. - Use a Windows version supported by .NET 10 on Windows (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/install/windows). Runtime support is not a claim that KillerBench repairs are validated on every supported edition or build. Windows 10 coverage remains pending and its supported editions are limited by Microsoft's current lifecycle requirements. - Obtain the Desktop Runtime only from Microsoft's .NET 10 download page (https://dotnet.microsoft.com/en-us/download/dotnet/10.0), choosing .NET Desktop Runtime , Windows , x64 . Follow your organization's software-installation rules. If dotnet is available, dotnet --list-runtimes should list Microsoft.WindowsDesktop.App 10.0.x for the x64 installation. A runtime installed only for another architecture does not satisfy the x64 installer package. When a package is published 1. Use the official download linked from this website. No KillerBench download link is active yet. 2. Read that package's requirements, GPLv3 license (../license.txt), Corresponding Source instructions, included third-party notices, signature and checksum information. A filename alone does not establish authenticity. 3. Run the installer for the current account, or for all users after an elevation prompt. It adds a Start menu shortcut, an optional desktop shortcut and an Add or Remove Programs entry. The portable edition runs without installation. 4. Start KillerBench as your normal account and follow Your first diagnosis . Diagnostic access failures stay visible; elevation is a separate deliberate choice. Do not bypass Windows security warnings to run an unverified copy. Keep a current backup before selecting any action that changes the machine. At startup KillerBench checks GitHub for a newer stable release and asks before installing it. The download must match the published SHA-256 checksum. Turn the check off with Check for updates on startup in About. Current limits The current build has twelve sections, read-only diagnosis, reports and individually selected repair surfaces. Native repair validation is still in progress. A working UI, successful build or fixture result does not establish repair behavior on a real computer. See How changes stay safe (safety.html development-status). The illustrative console on the landing page is synthetic output. Final application screenshots and the first public package are still pending. Source, license and support KillerBench is licensed under GPLv3. Source-delivery instructions and third-party font permissions remain pending for the first public package. Read About KillerBench for source availability, distribution notices, evidence privacy and support information. The Engine guide (../engine/) is for developers with an authorized source checkout; it does not imply that a public Engine package is available."},{"u":"help.html","a":"first-run","g":"Help","t":"Your first diagnosis","h":[["first-run","Your first diagnosis"],["first-run_run-the-scan","Run the scan"],["first-run_read-the-report","Read the report"],["first-run_choose-checks-yourself","Choose checks yourself"],["first-run_fix-what-you-found","Fix what you found"]],"x":"Your first diagnosis Start with Download and setup , then run a Full system scan. It collects diagnostic evidence and saves a local journal; it does not run repairs. A finding describes the inspected scope, not every possible fault on the computer. Run the scan 1. Open KillerBench. The Diagnostics section opens with Full system scan selected. 2. Leave Auto-repair confirmed issues turned off for your first run. 3. Press Run diagnosis . Progress appears in Raw output on the right while each check runs. 4. When it finishes, the output pane switches to the Readable report . Start without elevation. Some Windows evidence, such as BitLocker protectors and protected services, needs administrator rights. Those checks report unavailable evidence under a standard account. If you need that evidence, finish the current run, keep the report, then deliberately reopen KillerBench with Run as administrator . Elevation does not select or approve a repair. Read the report The report starts with counts for Needs review , Verified repairs , Incomplete and Checked , then a recommended next step. - What needs attention lists problems the evidence supports, most important first. - Collected information shows what was read, including evidence that was incomplete or unavailable. - Historical events are labeled as history. An error from last month is not treated as a current fault. Missing evidence never counts as healthy. If a check could not run, the report says so. Choose checks yourself Open Choose checks (advanced) to pick individual checks instead of the full scan. Each section in the app also has focused tools, described in the Sections pages of this guide. Fix what you found When the report recommends a fix, it names the section and tool to open. Read How changes stay safe before your first repair. Native validation is still in progress; a successful diagnosis does not establish that every offered repair has been tested on your Windows build. Keep backups and review the exact target and consequences before any change."},{"u":"help.html","a":"workspace","g":"Help","t":"The workspace","h":[["workspace","The workspace"],["workspace_sections","Sections"],["workspace_tool-options","Tool options"],["workspace_output","Output"],["workspace_activity-log","Activity log"],["workspace_themes-languages-and-shortcuts","Themes, languages and shortcuts"]],"x":"The workspace The KillerBench window has three panes that follow the way you work: pick a section on the left, set the tool's options in the center, and read the output on the right. An activity log can open under the options. Sections The sidebar on the left lists every tool in twelve sections: Diagnostics, Repair, Updates, Devices, Network, Apps, Maintenance, Onboarding, Security, BitLocker, Privacy and Storage. Drag its edge to resize it, or drag it past its minimum width to snap it closed. Tool options The center pane shows the selected section, its tool list and the options for the chosen tool. Options that could change the machine are always off by default. Press Run diagnosis to start the tool. When Auto-repair confirmed issues is turned on, the same button reads Run repair . Stop ends the run after the current command finishes. Output The output pane on the right has two views. - Raw output shows the technical evidence as it is collected, with original line numbers. It opens on its own while a tool runs. - Readable report shows the finished report for the last run. It replaces the raw output when the run finishes. Use the filter box in Raw output to show only matching lines. Export report saves the report with its source paths and evidence excerpts. Session sources lists every piece of evidence gathered in this session. Activity log The activity log under the tool options records what KillerBench did in this session. Themes, languages and shortcuts The rail between the sidebar and the tool options holds the theme picker, the language picker, the shortcut list and the activity log toggle. KillerBench has 13 themes and 20 languages. The smoked-glass console and readable reports follow the active theme. Confirmations and Open, Save, and Folder dialogs use the same family styling, including the 98SE theme. Choose the theme before opening a dialog to use that appearance throughout the workflow. Key Action --- --- F1 Keyboard shortcuts F2 Show or hide the sidebar F3 Show or hide the activity log Alt+Shift+G Film Grain F12 About KillerBench Ctrl+O Open evidence Esc Close an overlay or stop reading a file"},{"u":"help.html","a":"safety","g":"Help","t":"How changes stay safe","h":[["safety","How changes stay safe"],["safety_before-a-change","Before a change"],["safety_after-a-change","After a change"],["safety_if-something-is-interrupted","If something is interrupted"],["safety_what-killerbench-will-not-do","What KillerBench will not do"],["safety_development-status","Development status"]],"x":"How changes stay safe KillerBench treats diagnosis and change as different jobs. Diagnosis only reads. The full system scan never runs repairs. Select a specific repair or action, review its options and prerequisites, and confirm the proposed change. Before a change 1. Fresh evidence. The tool reads the current state again. A saved report or an old event never authorizes a change. 2. Exact eligibility. The change is offered only when the evidence matches the exact problem it fixes. Anything incomplete blocks it. 3. Your confirmation. Review the selected action in its confirmation dialog. Some actions, such as decrypting a drive, need a second confirmation. The dialog follows the active app theme; its appearance does not change the action's requirements. 4. Prerequisites. Administrator rights, restart state and ownership by policy or device management are checked. Settings that a policy owns are left alone. 5. A pending record. KillerBench writes down what it is about to do before it asks Windows to do it. 6. A final recheck. Everything is checked again immediately before the request, so a change in the meantime stops it. After a change - KillerBench reads the result back and reports Verified only when Windows shows the expected state. - If Windows returns success but the state did not change, the result says so and nothing is repeated. - Each change is saved in a journal under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\KillerBench . If something is interrupted Supported journaled actions retain a pending record when a request may have reached Windows and verification has not completed. Use the action's documented reconciliation path, or its matching -recover command, to inspect that record. Reconciliation is not an automatic rollback. Keep the journal when an outcome is uncertain, and do not manually repeat the change. Crash, cancellation and later-boot behavior still need action-specific native validation. What KillerBench will not do - Clear a TPM, delete protectors it did not create, or display recovery passwords. - Change a setting that group policy or device management controls. - Run a repair just because a historical event or imported report mentions a problem. Diagnostics and report analysis run locally. An action you select can contact an external destination, including software downloads or recovery-key backup to Active Directory or Entra ID. Review the target before confirming. Saved captures and raw evidence can include machine identity and source paths, so review them before sharing. Development status Repair coverage is still expanding. A successful build or simulated check does not establish native repair behavior. The repository's VM-VALIDATION.md tracks disposable-Windows-machine scenarios and remaining validation."},{"u":"help.html","a":"fix-windows-update","g":"Help","t":"Fix Windows Update","h":[["fix-windows-update","Fix Windows Update"],["fix-windows-update_before-you-start","Before you start"],["fix-windows-update_find-out-why-updates-fail","Find out why updates fail"],["fix-windows-update_watch-an-update-that-seems-stuck","Watch an update that seems stuck"],["fix-windows-update_repair-what-the-report-found","Repair what the report found"],["fix-windows-update_install-a-specific-update","Install a specific update"],["fix-windows-update_from-the-command-line","From the command line"],["fix-windows-update_related","Related"]],"x":"Fix Windows Update Use this when updates fail, stall or keep reinstalling. KillerBench reads the update chain first, explains the failure with the evidence behind it, and only starts a service or repairs the component store when fresh evidence proves that exact problem. Before you start - Run KillerBench as administrator. Update services, the component store and protected system files need elevation for complete evidence. - Restart first if Windows already has a restart pending. Many update repairs are blocked until restart readiness is clear. - Keep the computer on AC power for servicing work. Find out why updates fail 1. Open Updates and choose Windows Update in the tool sidebar. 2. Leave Auto-repair confirmed issues off for the first pass and press Run diagnosis . 3. Read the Readable report . It lists stopped update services, pending restarts, low free space, component-store corruption and the exact error codes Windows recorded, with a recommended next step. 4. If the report names an error code, the matching follow-up checks run automatically. You can also look one up yourself with killerbench-cli update-code 0x80070002 . Watch an update that seems stuck Choose Servicing activity monitor in Updates and press Run diagnosis . It takes repeated snapshots of update, Setup and Installer activity, transfer totals and restart indicators. Activity is shown as evidence only; it never claims a stall or a fault on its own. Repair what the report found 1. Run Windows Update again with Auto-repair confirmed issues turned on. It can start enabled update services that are confirmed stopped, and it runs DISM or SFC only when the live check confirms corruption. 2. For a single service, choose its focused tool instead, such as BITS service , Cryptographic Services , Windows Modules Installer or Update Orchestrator Service . Each one offers recovery only for that exact service. 3. When the report says to restart, restart before running anything else. KillerBench records restart-required outcomes and defers later work until the restart is done. Every repair is journaled and read back. See How changes stay safe for the full sequence. Install a specific update 1. Choose Available updates in Updates and press Run diagnosis to discover updates from the configured source. 2. Select the updates you want and press Preview selected . The preview lists exactly what would install and any restart requirement. 3. Install only after reading the preview. KillerBench never selects or approves an update for you. From the command line See update-search (cli.html cmd-update-search), update-history (cli.html cmd-update-history), servicing-monitor (cli.html cmd-servicing-monitor) and update-plan (cli.html cmd-update-plan). If an install was interrupted, run update-recover (cli.html cmd-update-recover); it checks the outcome and never retries the update. Related - Updates section - Repair system files - Troubleshooting"},{"u":"help.html","a":"repair-system-files","g":"Help","t":"Repair system files","h":[["repair-system-files","Repair system files"],["repair-system-files_before-you-start","Before you start"],["repair-system-files_check-for-corruption","Check for corruption"],["repair-system-files_repair-confirmed-corruption","Repair confirmed corruption"],["repair-system-files_clean-up-the-component-store","Clean up the component store"],["repair-system-files_from-the-command-line","From the command line"],["repair-system-files_related","Related"]],"x":"Repair system files Use this when Windows components are corrupted: features fail to install, updates report component errors, or system tools crash. KillerBench runs DISM and SFC only after a live check confirms corruption, then verifies the result. Before you start - Run KillerBench as administrator. - Clear any pending restart first. - If the machine is managed, check whether policy points DISM at a specific repair source. KillerBench reports that policy and does not change it. Check for corruption 1. Open Repair and choose System files . 2. Leave Auto-repair confirmed issues off and press Run diagnosis . 3. Read the report. It inventories servicing package, optional-feature and capability states, checks prerequisites and scans protected system files. A clean result means no repair is offered. Repair confirmed corruption 1. Turn on Auto-repair confirmed issues and press Run repair . KillerBench chooses DISM or SFC only when the live check confirms corruption. 2. Wait for verification. The report shows Verified only when the follow-up check reads back a repaired state. 3. If the report says the store is unrecoverable or the repair source is unavailable, stop there. Repeating the repair will not change the outcome. An interrupted DISM or SFC run is reconciled on the next boot without repeating the servicing command. Clean up the component store Choose Component-store cleanup in Updates . It first asks Windows whether cleanup is recommended. Optional maintenance removes superseded components and analyzes again. It never uses ResetBase and never deletes WinSxS content by hand. From the command line See component-repair-source (cli.html cmd-component-repair-source), repair (cli.html cmd-repair) and servicing-logs (cli.html cmd-servicing-logs). repair previews by default and acts only with --execute . Related - Fix Windows Update - Repair section (repair.html system-files) - How changes stay safe"},{"u":"help.html","a":"free-disk-space","g":"Help","t":"Free up disk space","h":[["free-disk-space","Free up disk space"],["free-disk-space_see-how-much-space-is-left","See how much space is left"],["free-disk-space_preview-a-cleanup","Preview a cleanup"],["free-disk-space_remove-the-files","Remove the files"],["free-disk-space_remove-the-previous-windows-installation","Remove the previous Windows installation"],["free-disk-space_from-the-command-line","From the command line"],["free-disk-space_related","Related"]],"x":"Free up disk space Use this when a drive is running low. KillerBench shows where space is going, previews what each cleanup target would remove, and deletes only after you turn on permanent cleanup. See how much space is left 1. Open Storage and choose Storage . 2. Press Run diagnosis . The report flags every drive-letter volume under 10% free space and shows shadow-copy allocation, quota defaults and Storage Sense policy. 3. For crash dumps and old update content, also run Vitals in Diagnostics and Windows Update in Updates ; the update cache size appears there. Preview a cleanup 1. Open Maintenance and choose Cleanup . 2. Tick the targets you want, such as Current user temporary files , Windows temporary files , Current user browser caches , Delivery Optimization cache or Current user Recycle Bin . Hover a target to read exactly which folders it covers. 3. Set Minimum age (days) and Maximum files per target . 4. Press Run diagnosis with Perform the selected permanent cleanup after fresh checks turned off. The report lists candidates and estimated space for each target. Nothing is deleted. Remove the files 1. Turn on Perform the selected permanent cleanup after fresh checks and press Run diagnosis . 2. KillerBench rechecks every candidate before removing it, skips files that are recent, protected or in use, and records each outcome in the journal. Permanent cleanup bypasses the Recycle Bin and cannot be undone. Removing error reports and crash dumps discards evidence you may need to investigate crashes, so review those first. Remove the previous Windows installation After a feature upgrade, Previous Windows installation in Cleanup can remove the rollback copy through the supported Windows rollback-removal request. It runs by itself, needs I backed up any files and settings I need from the previous installation , and permanently removes the ability to go back to the earlier version. From the command line See volume-capacity (cli.html cmd-volume-capacity), cleanup (cli.html cmd-cleanup) and previous-windows (cli.html cmd-previous-windows). Related - Maintenance section (maintenance.html cleanup) - Storage section - Check and repair a drive"},{"u":"help.html","a":"check-a-drive","g":"Help","t":"Check and repair a drive","h":[["check-a-drive","Check and repair a drive"],["check-a-drive_before-you-start","Before you start"],["check-a-drive_check-the-volume","Check the volume"],["check-a-drive_repair-the-filesystem","Repair the filesystem"],["check-a-drive_optimize-or-retrim","Optimize or retrim"],["check-a-drive_from-the-command-line","From the command line"],["check-a-drive_related","Related"]],"x":"Check and repair a drive Use this when a volume reports errors, Windows asks to scan a drive, or files fail to open. KillerBench checks the volume read-only first and only schedules or runs a filesystem repair after you confirm a backup. Before you start - Back up important data on the volume. A filesystem repair can change or remove damaged files. - Run KillerBench as administrator. Check the volume 1. Open Storage and choose Storage . 2. Pick the drive in Volume to check and press Run diagnosis . 3. Read the report. It shows disk and volume health, the dirty bit, online NTFS self-healing, controller and disk device errors, reliability counters and recent storage events such as resets, retries and unexpected removal. A clean check means no repair is offered. Disk temperature, wear and uncorrected I/O counters are review evidence and do not trigger a repair by themselves. Repair the filesystem 1. Press Review filesystem repair and choose Spot repair or Offline scan and repair . 2. For a data volume, tick Allow a non-system volume to go offline if you can close everything that uses it. The Windows volume cannot go offline, so its repair is scheduled for the next restart instead. 3. Tick Important data on this volume is backed up . 4. Confirm the repair. KillerBench rechecks the exact volume immediately before it acts and records a pending entry first. 5. For a scheduled repair, restart when convenient. KillerBench verifies the result after the restart without running the repair again. Optimize or retrim The same tool can analyze the volume and run the Windows-recommended optimization, Retrim solid-state storage or a hard-disk defragmentation. Each is a separate confirmed action on one exact healthy volume. From the command line See storage-check (cli.html cmd-storage-check), storage-repair-plan (cli.html cmd-storage-repair-plan), storage-repair (cli.html cmd-storage-repair) and volume-optimize (cli.html cmd-volume-optimize). Related - Storage section - Free up disk space - Troubleshooting"},{"u":"help.html","a":"fix-network","g":"Help","t":"Fix network and internet problems","h":[["fix-network","Fix network and internet problems"],["fix-network_check-the-connection","Check the connection"],["fix-network_repair-what-the-report-found","Repair what the report found"],["fix-network_wireless-wired-sign-in-and-proxies","Wireless, wired sign-in and proxies"],["fix-network_from-the-command-line","From the command line"],["fix-network_related","Related"]],"x":"Fix network and internet problems Use this when a computer has no internet, cannot reach a server or name, or keeps dropping its connection. KillerBench checks the adapter, address, gateway, DNS and firewall in order, and only renews a lease or starts a service when fresh evidence proves it is needed. Check the connection 1. Open Network and choose Network . 2. To test something specific, fill in Hostname lookup (optional) , or TCP destination (optional) and TCP port . Leave them blank to check only the local configuration. No application data is sent. 3. Set Timeout per lookup or connection (seconds) if the network is slow. 4. Leave Auto-repair confirmed issues off and press Run diagnosis . 5. Read the report. It separates a missing address, a missing or unreachable gateway, DNS configuration problems, proxy settings, firewall profile mismatches and a failed lookup or connection, and gives the next step for each. A reply from the gateway does not prove internet access, and a failed lookup through your configured resolver does not mean the site is down. Repair what the report found 1. Run Network again with Auto-repair confirmed issues turned on. It can start enabled DNS Client, DHCP Client, SMB client, Network Location Awareness, Network List and IP Helper services, and renew connected physical DHCP adapters that lack a usable IPv4 address. 2. Expect the connection to drop briefly during a renewal. Static addresses and virtual adapters are never changed. 3. For one bad cached name, use the DNS cache flush for that exact host instead of clearing everything. Routes, Hosts entries, DNS servers, firewall rules, Winsock providers and network profiles are always preserved. Wireless, wired sign-in and proxies - Wireless networking checks the adapter and WLAN AutoConfig, and can start the service if it is stopped. - Wired authentication (802.1X) does the same for Wired AutoConfig on networks that need sign-in at the port. - WinHTTP Auto-Proxy shows stored proxy settings for the machine and the current account. KillerBench never fetches a PAC URL or changes proxy settings during diagnosis. From the command line See workflow (cli.html cmd-workflow), dns-check (cli.html cmd-dns-check), tcp-check (cli.html cmd-tcp-check), dhcp-renew (cli.html cmd-dhcp-renew) and dns-cache (cli.html cmd-dns-cache). Related - Network section - Fix printing problems - How changes stay safe"},{"u":"help.html","a":"fix-printing","g":"Help","t":"Fix printing problems","h":[["fix-printing","Fix printing problems"],["fix-printing_check-the-printers","Check the printers"],["fix-printing_repair-the-queue","Repair the queue"],["fix-printing_clear-one-stuck-job","Clear one stuck job"],["fix-printing_fix-the-default-printer","Fix the default printer"],["fix-printing_from-the-command-line","From the command line"],["fix-printing_related","Related"]],"x":"Fix printing problems Use this when jobs sit in the queue, a printer shows offline, or the wrong printer is the default. KillerBench reads the spooler, queues, jobs, drivers and ports first, then lets you fix one exact queue or job. Check the printers 1. Open Devices and choose Printing . 2. Press Find local printers and pick the queue that is misbehaving. To check every queue, choose Use general printing checks . 3. Leave Auto-repair confirmed issues off and press Run diagnosis . 4. Read the report. It shows the Print Spooler state, paused queues, offline mode, visible jobs, driver conditions, port registrations, recent PrintService errors and, for network printers, one connection attempt to the printer's assigned address. A successful connection or a clean condition code does not prove the printer has paper, toner or a working mechanism. Repair the queue 1. Run Printing again with Auto-repair confirmed issues turned on. It can start the enabled spooler, resume an exact paused queue that has jobs waiting, and clear offline mode. 2. Clearing offline mode can release waiting jobs, so make sure those jobs should still print. 3. A queue someone paused on purpose with nothing waiting stays paused. Clear one stuck job 1. Press Find blocked print jobs . 2. Select the job that shows Error or Blocked and tick Cancel only this selected job . 3. Press Run diagnosis . Only that job is canceled, and the result is read back. Fix the default printer Choose the queue that should be the default and tick Repair a missing or conflicting default with this queue , then press Run diagnosis . This only acts when the default is missing or conflicting. From the command line See printer-list (cli.html cmd-printer-list), printer-check (cli.html cmd-printer-check), printer-repair (cli.html cmd-printer-repair), print-job-cancel (cli.html cmd-print-job-cancel) and default-printer-set (cli.html cmd-default-printer-set). Related - Devices section (devices.html printing) - Fix network and internet problems"},{"u":"help.html","a":"encrypt-with-bitlocker","g":"Help","t":"Encrypt a drive with BitLocker","h":[["encrypt-with-bitlocker","Encrypt a drive with BitLocker"],["encrypt-with-bitlocker_before-you-start","Before you start"],["encrypt-with-bitlocker_check-the-drive","Check the drive"],["encrypt-with-bitlocker_add-the-protectors-you-need","Add the protectors you need"],["encrypt-with-bitlocker_back-up-the-recovery-key-and-encrypt","Back up the recovery key and encrypt"],["encrypt-with-bitlocker_back-up-to-active-directory-or-entra-id","Back up to Active Directory or Entra ID"],["encrypt-with-bitlocker_data-drives-and-auto-unlock","Data drives and auto-unlock"],["encrypt-with-bitlocker_from-the-command-line","From the command line"],["encrypt-with-bitlocker_related","Related"]],"x":"Encrypt a drive with BitLocker Use this to turn on BitLocker, back up the recovery key and confirm the drive is protected. KillerBench backs up and verifies the recovery password before it starts encryption, and it never shows or logs a recovery password. Before you start - Run KillerBench as administrator. - The Windows drive needs a TPM protector and a recovery password protector. A data drive needs only a recovery password. - Decide where the recovery key backup goes: a file outside the drive you are encrypting, and optionally Active Directory or Microsoft Entra ID. Check the drive 1. Open BitLocker and choose the drive in Volume . 2. Leave the operation on Inspect and press Run diagnosis . The report shows encryption state, protection status and protector identities. 3. If the TPM is present but not ready, prepare it from Security first. KillerBench never clears a TPM. Add the protectors you need 1. Open More BitLocker actions . 2. Choose Add a TPM protector or Add a recovery password , press Preview , tick Make this change on the selected drive when I press Run action , then press Run action . 3. Back up a new recovery password straight away. KillerBench does not display it. Back up the recovery key and encrypt 1. Press Find recovery protectors and select the recovery password protector. 2. Enter a full backup file path outside the drive you are encrypting. 3. Choose Encrypt , pick the encryption method, and tick Back up the selected recovery key, then start encryption when I select Run diagnosis . 4. Press Run diagnosis . KillerBench writes the backup, verifies it, then starts encryption once. 5. Encryption continues in the background. Run Inspect later to confirm the drive reports fully encrypted with protection on. Back up to Active Directory or Entra ID In More BitLocker actions , choose the directory backup for the selected protector. KillerBench reports that Windows accepted the request but cannot read the directory copy, so confirm it in Active Directory or the Entra admin center. Data drives and auto-unlock After a data drive is encrypted, Turn on auto-unlock makes it unlock when Windows starts. The Windows drive must already be fully encrypted with protection on. From the command line See bitlocker-protectors (cli.html cmd-bitlocker-protectors), bitlocker-protector-add (cli.html cmd-bitlocker-protector-add), bitlocker-encrypt (cli.html cmd-bitlocker-encrypt) and bitlocker-escrow (cli.html cmd-bitlocker-escrow). Each has a matching -recover command for an interrupted request. Related - BitLocker section - Security section - How changes stay safe"},{"u":"help.html","a":"investigate-crashes","g":"Help","t":"Investigate crashes and blue screens","h":[["investigate-crashes","Investigate crashes and blue screens"],["investigate-crashes_check-the-computer","Check the computer"],["investigate-crashes_narrow-it-down","Narrow it down"],["investigate-crashes_read-crash-dumps","Read crash dumps"],["investigate-crashes_from-the-command-line","From the command line"],["investigate-crashes_related","Related"]],"x":"Investigate crashes and blue screens Use this when Windows restarts unexpectedly, shows a stop error, or an application keeps crashing. KillerBench gathers crash, shutdown, hardware-error and reliability evidence into one report. It describes what was recorded and never guesses a root cause. Check the computer 1. Open Diagnostics and choose Vitals . 2. Press Run diagnosis . The report combines memory, disk health, device problems, Reliability Monitor history, hardware-error records, crash reports, unexpected shutdowns and Windows Memory Diagnostic results. 3. Read the conclusion and the evidence under it. Historical crashes explain what happened; they do not prove a fault is still present. Narrow it down - Hardware error history reads WHEA and memory diagnostic records together with device problems and disk health. - Reliability history groups the last 14 days of application crashes, hangs and unexpected shutdowns. - Application errors focuses on crashing programs. - Driver installation history shows failed driver installs matched to devices that are present now. - Device problems lists devices with Device Manager error codes. Read crash dumps KillerBench lists dump configuration and dump-file metadata and can analyze minidump structure for the exception and module names. Dump contents are never opened in the desktop app. With a bug-check knowledge pack loaded, stop codes resolve to their documented meaning. Keep the dumps until you finish investigating. Cleanup can remove them, but that discards the evidence. From the command line See vitals-check (cli.html cmd-vitals-check), crash-review (cli.html cmd-crash-review) and device-code (cli.html cmd-device-code). Related - Diagnostics section (diagnostics.html vitals) - Reports and evidence"},{"u":"help.html","a":"make-windows-private","g":"Help","t":"Make Windows more private","h":[["make-windows-private","Make Windows more private"],["make-windows-private_review-the-current-settings","Review the current settings"],["make-windows-private_change-a-setting","Change a setting"],["make-windows-private_undo-a-change","Undo a change"],["make-windows-private_declutter-windows","Declutter Windows"],["make-windows-private_from-the-command-line","From the command line"],["make-windows-private_related","Related"]],"x":"Make Windows more private Use this to review and tighten privacy on a laptop. KillerBench reads the current settings, shows which ones policy or device management already own, and changes only the settings you pick, one at a time, with an exact undo. Review the current settings 1. Open Privacy . 2. Tick the areas you want to review. Every area starts unchecked. 3. Press Run diagnosis . The report shows the current value for this machine and the account running KillerBench, and flags settings owned by policy. Change a setting 1. Choose the setting in Privacy setting , such as camera, microphone, location, diagnostic data, advertising, clipboard history, cloud content search or search highlights. 2. Choose the new value and tick Apply when I select Run diagnosis . 3. Press Run diagnosis . KillerBench checks for a policy owner, records the current value, makes the one change and reads it back. Settings owned by group policy or device management are left alone. Change those where they are managed. Undo a change Each verified change keeps a journal. The matching undo restores the exact previous value, again after a policy check. Declutter Windows For suggestions, consumer content, Recall and AI features, taskbar and Start clutter, Edge and preinstalled software, open Maintenance and choose Declutter Windows . The review is read-only and nothing is selected automatically. From the command line See privacy-plan (cli.html cmd-privacy-plan), privacy-setting (cli.html cmd-privacy-setting), privacy-setting-undo (cli.html cmd-privacy-setting-undo) and debloat-review (cli.html cmd-debloat-review). An unsupported key lists the supported choices. Related - Privacy section - Set up a new computer"},{"u":"help.html","a":"set-up-new-pc","g":"Help","t":"Set up a new computer","h":[["set-up-new-pc","Set up a new computer"],["set-up-new-pc_start-with-a-baseline","Start with a baseline"],["set-up-new-pc_review-applications-and-configuration","Review applications and configuration"],["set-up-new-pc_name-the-computer","Name the computer"],["set-up-new-pc_protect-and-tidy-it","Protect and tidy it"],["set-up-new-pc_from-the-command-line","From the command line"],["set-up-new-pc_related","Related"]],"x":"Set up a new computer Use this when preparing a new or freshly reset PC. KillerBench reviews the computer, the applications you plan to install and the name you want to give it, then points to the guarded tools that make each change. Start with a baseline 1. Run Full system scan from Diagnostics and keep the report as a record of the computer's starting state. 2. Install pending updates. See Fix Windows Update if any fail. Review applications and configuration 1. Open Onboarding and choose Onboarding . 2. Select the applications and configuration groups you plan to set up and press Run diagnosis . The review compares them with what is already installed. KillerBench does not download or run installers. 3. Before running a vendor installer, verify it from the command line. KillerBench trusts it only when it carries a valid signature from that vendor's exact organization name. Name the computer 1. Choose Computer name , enter the name and press Run diagnosis to review it against the current identity and domain role. 2. On a workgroup computer, tick Rename this workgroup PC after fresh checks to rename it. A restart is needed to finish. Protect and tidy it - Encrypt a drive with BitLocker and back up the recovery key. - Make Windows more private and review Declutter Windows in Maintenance . - Create a restore point with Prepare one restore point once everything is in place. From the command line See workflow (cli.html cmd-workflow) and restore-point (cli.html cmd-restore-point). Related - Onboarding section - Your first diagnosis"},{"u":"help.html","a":"use-the-cli","g":"Help","t":"Run KillerBench from scripts","h":[["use-the-cli","Run KillerBench from scripts"],["use-the-cli_get-started","Get started"],["use-the-cli_run-a-scan-and-save-a-report","Run a scan and save a report"],["use-the-cli_read-exit-codes","Read exit codes"],["use-the-cli_make-a-change","Make a change"],["use-the-cli_reopen-old-results","Reopen old results"],["use-the-cli_use-it-from-an-ai-assistant","Use it from an AI assistant"],["use-the-cli_related","Related"]],"x":"Run KillerBench from scripts Use the command line when you want KillerBench evidence in a script, an RMM job or a remote console. killerbench-cli runs the same Engine as the desktop app and prints JSON. Get started 1. Open PowerShell where killerbench-cli.exe is, or add its folder to your path. 2. Run killerbench-cli help to list every command with its safety boundary. 3. Run killerbench-cli diagnostic-scopes to see the 11 recommended diagnostic groups. Run a scan and save a report full-scan is diagnostic-only and rejects --auto-repair . Use a focused repair workflow when a specific action is needed. --report writes the same themed report the app exports. Use --scope to check only some areas, and common-check for combined JSON without a workflow journal. Read exit codes Code Meaning --- --- 0 The preview or verified result succeeded. 1 A startup, input or journal error stopped the command. 2 The arguments were not recognized. 3 Blocked, failed or unverified. 4 A workflow needs a restart before it can verify. 130 You stopped it. Exit 0 on a read-only command means it ran, not that the computer is healthy. Read the JSON for findings. Make a change Every command that can change the machine previews by default. Read the preview, then run the same command with --execute . If a request is interrupted, run the matching -recover command; it reports the real outcome and never repeats the change. Reopen old results Use it from an AI assistant KillerMCP can call the installed CLI to look up Device Manager and Windows error codes. Related - Command line reference - Engine developer guide - Troubleshooting"},{"u":"help.html","a":"diagnostics","g":"Help","t":"Diagnostics","h":[["diagnostics","Diagnostics"],["diagnostics_tools-in-this-section","Tools in this section"],["diagnostics_full-system-scan","Full system scan"],["diagnostics_vitals","Vitals"],["diagnostics_reliability-history","Reliability history"],["diagnostics_startup-performance","Startup performance"],["diagnostics_hardware-error-history","Hardware error history"],["diagnostics_driver-installation-history","Driver installation history"],["diagnostics_system-restore-points","System Restore points"],["diagnostics_windows-recovery-environment","Windows Recovery Environment"],["diagnostics_windows-activation","Windows activation"],["diagnostics_windows-services","Windows services"],["diagnostics_application-errors","Application errors"],["diagnostics_display-devices","Display devices"],["diagnostics_input-devices","Input devices"],["diagnostics_device-problems","Device problems"]],"x":"Diagnostics Diagnostics is where every visit should start. Each tool reads the machine and writes a report. Nothing here changes Windows. The default tool, the full system scan, runs a broad set of read-only checks and ranks what needs attention. Step-by-step guides: Investigate crashes and blue screens and Repair system files . Tools in this section Check the machine first, then read the report for what needs attention and the next safe step. Full system scan Inspect 49 diagnostic areas and evaluate installed repair recipes for current evidence. Checks can take several minutes. Full system scan is diagnostic-only. Use a focused repair workflow to select and confirm one supported action after reviewing fresh prerequisites. Historical, backup and transfer snapshots remain read-only. Dump contents are not opened. Dirty-bit inspection does not schedule repair or restart. This scan does not install updates, change drivers, delete files or perform a complete hardware test. Vitals Capture available memory, provider-reported disk health and BitLocker protection status, then correlate current vitals with device problems, reliability history, hardware-error records, crash evidence, unexpected shutdowns and Windows Memory Diagnostic results. Restart indicators, system disk free space, elevation and update service configuration remain visible. Recovery passwords are not collected. No repairs run in this workflow. The combined result is not a complete hardware, software or stability test. Reliability history Read the last 14 days of Windows Reliability Monitor records together with recent Application errors and unexpected shutdown events, then return one bounded historical conclusion. Historical records do not establish current health or root cause. No repair is selected and no applications, services or settings are changed. Startup performance Read the last 14 days of Windows startup and shutdown performance event metadata, retaining only provider identity and bounded numeric duration fields, then correlate it with supported pending restart indicators. Paths, process names, accounts and other event payload are removed before evidence is saved. Historical degradation records do not identify a current bottleneck or authorize startup, service, application, driver, task, power, restart or policy changes. Hardware error history Read the last 14 days of Windows Hardware Error Architecture and Memory Diagnostic history, current Plug and Play device problems, physical-disk health and provider reliability counters, then return one bounded hardware conclusion. Records require correlation with their payload, firmware diagnostics and vendor hardware tests. No failing component or root cause is inferred, no repair is selected, and no hardware, firmware, driver, disk, pool, volume, file, service, power, restart or setting is changed. Driver installation history Read the retained tail of Windows SetupAPI device-install history, correlate failed sections with exact current Plug and Play identities and return one bounded conclusion. Historical log sections do not prove a device is still present or a failure is current. No driver is installed, removed, rolled back or updated. System Restore points List up to 256 available System Restore points, review explicit creation eligibility and return one bounded readiness conclusion. The inventory does not prove that protection is enabled for every volume or that a restore will succeed. No restore point is created, deleted or applied during diagnosis. Windows Recovery Environment Read the current Windows Recovery Environment configuration with REAgentC, correlate an enabled local location with the exact current disk and partition, and return one bounded readiness conclusion. Optional automatic repair can enable a disabled existing configuration, then verify its reported state. The recovery image is not downloaded, relocated or replaced, no recovery boot is initiated, and Windows is not restarted. Enabled status and partition presence do not prove that the recovery image can boot or repair this PC. Windows activation Read current Windows license status without collecting full or partial product keys, retain the last seven days of sanitized Software Protection warning, error and critical event metadata, then return one bounded operational conclusion. Event payload is removed before evidence is saved. Historical records remain context when current licensing reports Licensed. No activation, product-key, token, license, account, service or policy action is selected. Provider status and event history do not prove subscription entitlement, activation-server reachability, token-store integrity, future validity or root cause. Windows services Inventory installed service names, display names, states, start modes and privacy-bounded recovery action types without collecting accounts, executable paths, recovery commands or reboot messages. Review startup-mode coherence, direct dependency identities and cycles, repeat exact pending states, inspect supported services and dependencies of stopped services, then correlate recent Service Control Manager failures only when one exact service name or unique display name is present. Load-order group use is counted without expanding membership, and ambiguous identities remain unresolved. Stopped demand-start services can be normal. No service is started and startup, dependency and recovery settings are preserved. Use the corresponding Repair workflow when a specific feature needs recovery. Application errors Read recent Application error and critical events together with bounded Reliability Monitor history, then return one stability-history conclusion. Historical records do not prove a current fault or root cause and only direct narrower diagnosis. No applications are stopped, repaired, removed or reconfigured. Display devices Inspect graphics adapters and monitors for reported device errors, hardware IDs and installed driver versions. No driver in"},{"u":"help.html","a":"repair","g":"Help","t":"Repair","h":[["repair","Repair"],["repair_automatic-and-manual-repair","Automatic and manual repair"],["repair_tools-in-this-section","Tools in this section"],["repair_core-windows-services","Core Windows services"],["repair_system-files","System files"],["repair_wmi-repository","WMI repository"],["repair_windows-system-notifications","Windows system notifications"],["repair_diagnostic-policy-service","Diagnostic Policy Service"],["repair_windows-error-reporting","Windows Error Reporting"],["repair_windows-event-collector","Windows Event Collector"],["repair_windows-event-log","Windows Event Log"],["repair_windows-time-service","Windows Time service"],["repair_task-scheduler-service","Task Scheduler service"],["repair_user-profile-service","User Profile Service"],["repair_backup-services","Backup services"]],"x":"Repair Repair collects the tools that can fix something. Each one diagnoses first. A repair is offered only when fresh evidence shows the exact problem it fixes, and it runs only after you turn on Auto-repair confirmed issues or confirm a single repair yourself. Every repair is journaled and verified afterwards. Step-by-step guides: Repair system files and Fix Windows Update . Automatic and manual repair Auto-repair confirmed issues lets a diagnostic tool run the repairs it proves are needed after the scan. It is off by default. For a single servicing repair, open Advanced: logs and manual repairs and choose Repairs . Pick Component store (DISM) or System files (SFC) , read the preview, tick Run this repair on this PC and press Run repair . See How changes stay safe for what happens before and after any change. Tools in this section Use this after a diagnosis identifies a problem. Repairs require confirmed evidence and explicit approval. Core Windows services Check Power, Plug and Play, User Manager, Group Policy Client, Network Store Interface, RPC Endpoint Mapper, Remote Procedure Call, DCOM Server Process Launcher, Background Tasks Infrastructure and CoreMessaging services together. Optional recovery starts only exact enabled automatic services that fresh evidence confirms are stopped after the startup window and complete dependency checks. Demand-start services remain unchanged when idle. Power plans, devices, drivers, accounts, credentials, profiles, policy, adapters, addresses, routes, DNS, firewall configuration, registrations, endpoints, task registrations, application data, service permissions and startup types are preserved. No policy refresh, device scan, account action, network change, power-setting change, registration change or task launch is requested. Verification covers stable service state only, not end-to-end feature behavior. System files Inventory bounded Windows servicing package, optional-feature and capability states, check prerequisites, scan protected system files, and automatically choose SFC only when the live check confirms corruption. Pending package, feature or capability states request review but do not authorize installation, removal, cleanup, rollback, enablement or disablement. WMI repository Check WMI service availability, then repository consistency. Auto-repair can start an eligible stopped service and salvage confirmed corruption, then verify each result. Disabled service settings are preserved. Readable registrations are merged; unreadable content may require provider reinstallation. Management clients may be interrupted. No reset, deletion or automatic rollback. Windows system notifications Read recent Service Control Manager history, then check COM+ Event System and System Event Notification Service together. Optional recovery verifies COM+ Event System readiness before starting SENS. Existing logon, network, power and COM+ notifications may resume under current configuration. COM+ catalogs, subscriptions, notification registrations, consumers, accounts, permissions, recovery settings, policy and startup types are preserved. No subscription, registration, consumer, account, permission or policy is changed. Verification covers stable service state only, not end-to-end notification delivery. Diagnostic Policy Service Review bounded Diagnostic Policy operational history, exact service-failure history and current service readiness. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. Historical records cannot authorize service recovery or a troubleshooting action. Optional recovery starts only the enabled, stopped exact service after fresh dependency checks. Existing diagnostic scenarios may resume. Policy, troubleshooting data and startup type are preserved. No troubleshooter or suggested fix is run. Verification covers stable service state only, not fault detection, diagnosis completion or repair outcome. Windows Error Reporting Review crash-dump configuration, bounded dump-file metadata, recent system crash reports, exact Windows Error Reporting service-failure history and current service readiness. Historical reports do not establish a current fault or root cause and cannot authorize recovery. Optional recovery starts only the enabled, stopped exact service after fresh dependency checks. Existing queued reports may resume under current policy. Report archives, dumps, consent, upload policy and startup type are preserved. No upload or deletion is requested. Verification covers stable service state only, not report creation, upload, delivery, problem resolution or crash diagnosis. Windows Event Collector Review bounded Windows Event Collector subscription inventory, exact service-failure history and current service readiness. An empty inventory can be intentional, and subscription count or historical failures cannot authorize recovery. Optional recovery starts only the enabled, stopped exact service after fresh dependency checks. Existing subscriptions may resume remote event collection. Subscriptions, forwarded events, credentials, channel settings, policy and startup type are preserved. Verification covers stable service state only, not source reachability, authentication, remote delivery, event freshness or subscription health. Windows Event Log Inspect four diagnostic channels for enabled state, retention mode, current size, configured limit, record count and saturation, then check Windows Event Log service availability. Auto-repair can start a stopped, enabled service after checking dependencies and verify running state. A separately confirmed focused action can enable one exact disabled unmanaged diagnostic channel while preserving every other reported setting. Logs, channel permissions, retention, size limits, startup types and managed settings are preserved. Successful event delivery and archive capacity remain unverified. Windows Time service Check Windows Time configuration, service and synchronization. Auto-repair can start W32Time when the current sta"},{"u":"help.html","a":"updates","g":"Help","t":"Updates","h":[["updates","Updates"],["updates_tools-in-this-section","Tools in this section"],["updates_servicing-activity-monitor","Servicing activity monitor"],["updates_available-updates","Available updates"],["updates_windows-update","Windows Update"],["updates_windows-update-service","Windows Update Service"],["updates_delivery-optimization","Delivery Optimization"],["updates_bits-service","BITS service"],["updates_background-transfers-bits","Background transfers (BITS)"],["updates_cryptographic-services","Cryptographic Services"],["updates_windows-modules-installer","Windows Modules Installer"],["updates_windows-update-medic-service","Windows Update Medic Service"],["updates_update-orchestrator-service","Update Orchestrator Service"],["updates_component-store-cleanup","Component-store cleanup"]],"x":"Updates Updates covers Windows Update, the services behind it and the component store. You can see what is installed, what is pending and why an update fails, and you can review available updates before choosing any to install. Step-by-step guides: Fix Windows Update and Repair system files . Tools in this section Watch servicing activity, review available updates and repair the Windows Update chain. Servicing activity monitor Observe bounded Windows Update and Setup servicing activity over one or more snapshots. Options control refresh timing and whether update events, update history, the exact Windows Update scheduled task, exact service states, restart indicators, current-account background transfers, Delivery Optimization activity and privacy-bounded Windows Installer activity are included. Process IDs, command lines, users and paths are excluded. Activity does not prove progress, completion, a stall or a fault. No update, setup, transfer, task, service, process, policy, restart or repair action is requested. Available updates Discover selected software and driver updates using the configured source, Windows Update or an already registered Microsoft Update service. Source readiness is checked before discovery. No installation, license acceptance, source registration or policy changes. This is separate from servicing repair. Windows Update Inventory bounded Windows servicing package, optional-feature and capability states, check restart indicators, free space and update services, observe Delivery Optimization without starting it, then scan the component store and protected system files. Pending package, feature or capability states request review but do not authorize a component action. Auto-repair can start enabled Windows Update, BITS, Cryptographic Services, Windows Modules Installer and Update Orchestrator services. Starting Update Orchestrator may resume existing scheduled update activity under current policy. Packages, optional features, capabilities, Delivery Optimization cache, peer, bandwidth, policy, source enrollment, schedules and startup settings are preserved. DISM or SFC requires live evidence of corruption. No update is selected or approved by this workflow. Windows Update Service Check exact Windows Update Service state, its registered service DLL, bounded current update activity and exact service-failure history. DLL observations, update activity and historical failures cannot authorize a service start or update operation. Optional recovery starts only a stopped automatic service after fresh dependency and executable-registration checks. A stopped demand-start service remains informational. Existing update activity may resume under current policy. Update policy, source enrollment, schedules, restart settings, packages, BITS jobs, service permissions and startup type are preserved. KillerBench selects, approves or installs no update. Verification covers stable service state only, not source reachability, applicability, download, installation, completion or restart readiness. Delivery Optimization Check the exact Delivery Optimization service state. A stopped demand-start service remains informational. Only an exact Paused state can offer recovery. Optional recovery repeats the state check, sends one bounded continue request and requires two coherent Running observations. Startup type, cache contents, peers, bandwidth, update source and managed policy are preserved. Transfer activity, cache health, update progress and completion remain unverified. BITS service Check the exact Background Intelligent Transfer Service state, current-account transfer jobs and bounded exact service-failure history. Historical failures and transfer snapshots cannot authorize a service start. Optional recovery starts only the enabled, stopped exact service after fresh dependency checks. Existing applications may resume queued background transfers. Jobs, priorities, owners, destinations, proxy settings, policy and startup type are preserved. Verification covers stable service state only, not transfer progress, application ownership or completion. Background transfers (BITS) Read BITS transfer jobs in the selected account scope and review exact job identities. Queue names do not establish application ownership. No transfers are resumed, completed, canceled, deleted or reassigned. Cryptographic Services Check exact Cryptographic Services state, its registered service DLL and bounded exact service-failure history. Historical failures and DLL observations cannot authorize a service start or component replacement. Optional recovery starts only the enabled, stopped exact service after fresh dependency checks. Existing catalog, certificate and update operations may resume. Certificates, keys, catalogs, trust settings, policy and startup type are preserved. Verification covers stable service state only, not certificate, signature, catalog or trust validation. Windows Modules Installer Check exact Windows Modules Installer state, bounded servicing package transitions and exact service-failure history. Historical failures and package states cannot authorize a service start or package operation. Optional recovery starts only the enabled, stopped exact service after fresh dependency checks. Existing servicing activity may resume. Packages, component-store content, sources, policy and startup type are preserved. Verification covers stable service state only, not component-store health, package applicability, servicing completion or restart readiness. Windows Update Medic Service Check exact Windows Update Medic Service state, its registered service DLL, bounded current update activity and exact service-failure history. DLL observations, update activity and historical failures cannot authorize a service start or component action. A stopped demand-start service can be normal. When this exact tool is selected, optional recovery can start only the stopped enabled service after fresh dependency checks. Windows "},{"u":"help.html","a":"devices","g":"Help","t":"Devices","h":[["devices","Devices"],["devices_tools-in-this-section","Tools in this section"],["devices_printing","Printing"],["devices_audio","Audio"],["devices_camera-support","Camera support"],["devices_bluetooth-support","Bluetooth support"],["devices_smart-card-support","Smart card support"],["devices_biometric-support","Biometric support"],["devices_human-interface-device-service","Human Interface Device Service"],["devices_portable-device-enumerator-service","Portable Device Enumerator Service"],["devices_windows-image-acquisition","Windows Image Acquisition"],["devices_shell-hardware-detection","Shell Hardware Detection"],["devices_device-install-service","Device Install Service"],["devices_device-association-service","Device Association Service"],["devices_display-enhancement-service","Display Enhancement Service"],["devices_text-input-management-service","Text Input Management Service"],["devices_windows-font-cache-service","Windows Font Cache Service"],["devices_function-discovery-provider-host","Function Discovery Provider Host"],["devices_function-discovery-resource-publication","Function Discovery Resource Publication"]],"x":"Devices Devices covers printers, audio, cameras, Bluetooth, input and the Windows services that attached hardware relies on. Step-by-step guides: Fix printing problems and Investigate crashes and blue screens . Tools in this section Printing, audio, cameras, Bluetooth, input and the services behind attached hardware. Printing Check the Print Spooler, local queue pauses, visible print jobs, offline settings and driver-reported conditions, then inventory queue, driver, port, Standard TCP/IP endpoint, print processor and print monitor registrations. Correlate recent PrintService problems only when one exact current queue identity is present. Assigned Standard TCP/IP endpoints receive one bounded connection attempt without application data, SNMP or a print job. Auto-repair can start the enabled spooler, resume only exact paused queues with fresh waiting-job evidence, and clear offline mode. Idle intentional pauses remain review-only. Remote connections, SNMP community strings, extension registrations and job contents are preserved. A condition code or successful connection does not establish printer identity, supplies, mechanics, queue acceptance, rendering success or physical printing. Audio Check the default multimedia input and output identities and shared formats, audio device and driver state, Audio Endpoint Builder and Windows Audio. Auto-repair starts stopped, enabled services and verifies running state. Startup settings, drivers, selected devices, exclusive mode and volume levels are preserved. Sound playback, recording and application routing remain untested. Camera support Inspect camera and imaging device errors, hardware IDs, installed driver evidence and Windows Camera Frame Server availability. Optional recovery starts only an enabled, stopped service after dependency checks. An application already requesting camera access may resume under existing permissions. Camera privacy settings, permissions, device configuration, policy, drivers and startup type are preserved. No reset, permission change or driver installation. Verification covers reported device configuration and service running state only, not image capture, privacy access or physical operation. Bluetooth support Inspect Bluetooth device errors, hardware IDs, installed driver evidence and Bluetooth Support Service availability. Optional recovery starts only an enabled, stopped service after dependency checks; paired devices may reconnect. Pairings, radio settings, device configuration, policy and startup type are preserved. No reset, device removal or driver installation. Verification covers reported device configuration and service running state only, not discovery, pairing, audio or data transfer. Smart card support Inspect smart-card reader errors, hardware IDs, installed driver evidence and Smart Card service availability. Optional recovery starts only an enabled, stopped service after dependency checks. Applications may resume detecting inserted cards under existing policy. Certificates, PINs, credentials, reader and card configuration, policy, drivers and startup type are preserved. No initialization, enrollment, PIN operation, reader reset or driver installation. Verification covers reported reader configuration and service running state only, not card detection, authentication or application access. Biometric support Inspect biometric device errors, hardware IDs, installed driver evidence and Windows Biometric Service availability. Optional recovery starts only an enabled, stopped service after dependency checks. Existing Windows Hello and application requests may resume under current policy. Biometric templates, Windows Hello data, credentials, device configuration, permissions, policy, drivers and startup type are preserved. No enrollment, template deletion, sensor reset, permission change or driver installation. Verification covers reported sensor configuration and service running state only, not capture, matching, sign-in or application access. Human Interface Device Service Check Human Interface Device Service availability. Optional recovery starts only the enabled, stopped exact service after fresh dependency checks. Existing hot buttons, media controls and other HID features may resume. Devices, drivers, button mappings, accessibility settings, input settings, policy, service permissions and startup type are preserved. KillerBench selects no device, driver, mapping or input setting operation. Verification covers stable service state only, not button, control or application behavior. Portable Device Enumerator Service Check Portable Device Enumerator Service availability. Optional recovery starts only the enabled, stopped exact service after fresh dependency checks. Existing applications may resume detecting and accessing connected portable devices. Devices, drivers, files, libraries, synchronization settings, permissions, policy, service permissions and startup type are preserved. KillerBench selects no device, file, library, synchronization or transfer operation. Verification covers stable service state only, not enumeration, access, synchronization or transfer. Windows Image Acquisition Check Windows Image Acquisition service availability. Optional recovery starts only the enabled, stopped exact service after fresh dependency checks. Existing scanner and still-image acquisition requests may resume under current application permissions. Devices, drivers, image data, application permissions, privacy settings, transfer settings, policy, service permissions and startup type are preserved. KillerBench selects no device, driver, image, permission, transfer or acquisition operation. Verification covers stable service state only, not scanner, camera, transfer or application operation. Shell Hardware Detection Inspect bounded storage-controller, disk and portable-device Plug and Play evidence and installed driver references, then check Shell Hardware Detection service availability. Optional recovery starts only"},{"u":"help.html","a":"network","g":"Help","t":"Network","h":[["network","Network"],["network_tools-in-this-section","Tools in this section"],["network_network-2","Network"],["network_wireless-networking","Wireless networking"],["network_wired-authentication-802-1x","Wired authentication (802.1X)"],["network_network-connections","Network Connections"],["network_winhttp-auto-proxy","WinHTTP Auto-Proxy"],["network_windows-connection-manager","Windows Connection Manager"],["network_network-setup-service","Network Setup Service"],["network_file-sharing-smb","File sharing (SMB)"],["network_remote-access-connection-manager","Remote Access Connection Manager"],["network_remote-desktop-services","Remote Desktop Services"],["network_windows-remote-management","Windows Remote Management"]],"x":"Network Network inspects connections, DNS, routes, wireless and wired sign-in, file sharing and remote access. Tools that reach another host use the target and timeout you set, and nothing is changed unless you confirm a repair. Step-by-step guides: Fix network and internet problems . Tools in this section Inspect connections, DNS, routes, wireless, sharing and remote access. Network Inspect adapters, address state and origin, gateways, DNS configuration, effective DNS namespace policy, TCP settings, native Winsock protocol and namespace-provider catalog structure, the IP routing table and Hosts file mappings. Auto-repair can start enabled DNS Client, DHCP Client, SMB client, Network Location Awareness, Network List and IP Helper services and renew connected physical DHCP adapters lacking a usable IPv4 address. Starting network-identification services may recalculate network identity and apply a different existing firewall profile. IP Helper may activate existing IPv6 transition, port-proxy and network-notification features. Service recovery verifies running state, not successful name resolution, route selection, network identification or tunnel operation. Routes, Hosts entries, DNS servers, profiles, firewall rules, Winsock providers, transition configuration, startup types and policies are preserved. Renewal may change leased addresses and interrupt connections. Static settings and virtual adapters are preserved. Exact gateway ICMP responses do not verify Internet reachability. Wireless networking Inspect network devices and WLAN AutoConfig availability. Optional recovery starts only an enabled, stopped service after dependency checks; saved profiles may reconnect automatically. Profiles, credentials, policy, radio settings and startup types are preserved. No adapter reset or driver installation. Verification covers running state, not wireless authentication or connectivity. Wired authentication (802.1X) Inspect network devices and Wired AutoConfig for networks requiring 802.1X. Optional recovery starts only an enabled, stopped service after dependency checks. Existing authentication profiles may activate and change network access. Certificates, credentials, profiles, policy and startup settings are preserved. Verification covers running state only, not authentication or connectivity. Network Connections Review bounded current network profile assignments, exact service-failure history and current Network Connections service readiness. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. Public profiles and historical failures remain context and cannot authorize recovery or a network change. Optional recovery starts only the enabled, stopped exact service after fresh dependency checks. Existing network-management interfaces may resume. Adapters, addresses, routes, DNS settings, connection profiles, credentials, firewall configuration, policy and startup type are preserved. No connection or network setting is changed. Verification covers stable service state only, not adapter operation, address assignment, routing, name resolution or connectivity. WinHTTP Auto-Proxy Review stored WinHTTP defaults, current-account Internet settings, exact service-failure history and current WinHTTP Auto-Proxy readiness. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. Proxy configuration and historical failures cannot authorize service recovery or discovery. A stopped demand-start service can be normal. When this exact tool is selected, optional recovery can start only the stopped enabled service after fresh dependency checks. Existing applications may resume WPAD or PAC discovery under current settings. Proxy definitions, PAC URLs, credentials, network settings, firewall configuration, policy, service permissions and startup type are preserved. No URL is fetched and no proxy or network setting is changed. Verification covers stable service state only, not discovery, reachability, authentication, policy ownership or application use. Windows Connection Manager Review bounded current network profile assignments, exact service-failure history and current Windows Connection Manager readiness. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. Public profiles and historical failures remain context and cannot authorize recovery or a connection decision. Optional recovery starts only the enabled, stopped exact service after fresh dependency checks. Windows may resume automatic connection decisions under existing profiles and policy. Adapters, profiles, credentials, metering, cost, roaming, firewall, VPN and authentication settings and startup type are preserved. No profile, adapter or setting is changed. Verification covers stable service state only, not profile applicability, connection selection, authentication, routing, name resolution or connectivity. Network Setup Service Review bounded current adapter protocol bindings, exact service-failure history and current Network Setup Service readiness. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. Binding state and historical failures cannot authorize service recovery or a setup change. Optional recovery starts only the enabled, stopped exact service after fresh dependency checks. Existing pending network setup may resume under Windows policy. Adapters, drivers, driver packages, bindings, addresses, routes, DNS settings, profiles, credentials, firewall configuration, policy and startup type are preserved. KillerBench selects no driver or configuration change. Verification covers stable service state only, not adapter availability, driver suitability, setup completion, address assignment, routing, name resolution or connectivity. File sharing (SMB) Check Windows SMB server availability. Optional recovery starts only an enabled, stopped Server service after fresh dependency checks. Existing file and printer shares may become reachable under their current permissions and firewall policy. No share, permission, SMB setting, firewall rule, credential, policy or startup type is changed. Verification covers stable serv"},{"u":"help.html","a":"apps","g":"Help","t":"Apps","h":[["apps","Apps"],["apps_tools-in-this-section","Tools in this section"],["apps_installed-applications","Installed applications"],["apps_app-packages","App packages"],["apps_browser-extensions","Browser extensions"],["apps_startup-applications","Startup applications"],["apps_microsoft-app-removal","Microsoft app removal"],["apps_outlook-reset","Outlook reset"],["apps_foxit-inventory","Foxit inventory"],["apps_windows-installer-service","Windows Installer service"],["apps_windows-search","Windows Search"],["apps_app-readiness","App Readiness"],["apps_state-repository-service","State Repository Service"],["apps_appx-deployment-service","AppX Deployment Service"],["apps_client-license-service","Client License Service"],["apps_microsoft-store-install-service","Microsoft Store Install Service"],["apps_windows-license-manager-service","Windows License Manager Service"],["apps_windows-pushtoinstall-service","Windows PushToInstall Service"]],"x":"Apps Apps reviews installed programs, Store packages, Office and Outlook, browser extensions, startup apps and search. Removal and reset tools always show a review first and change only what you select. Step-by-step guides: Set up a new computer . Tools in this section Review installed applications, Store packages, Office and Outlook, browser extensions and search. Installed applications Read application uninstall registrations in the selected machine or running-account scope, then combine that scope with bounded Application error, Reliability Monitor and restart evidence. Other accounts and portable apps are excluded. Registrations and broad history are not exact application-health or ownership proof. No installer, uninstaller, application, process, service, file, registration, account, policy or restart action is selected. App packages Inventory registered Appx/MSIX packages for the selected account scope. Running account is the default; all users requires administrator access. Report exact identities, framework and non-removable flags, and dependency counts. Provisioned packages, Win32 applications, managed ownership and application data are not evaluated. No packages are removed. Browser extensions Inventory bounded Chrome, Edge, Brave and Firefox extension identities from the running account. Browser history, credentials, extension settings, manifest contents, commands and policy values are excluded. Presence does not prove enablement, trust, ownership or safe removal. No browser, extension or policy is changed. Startup applications Inventory machine and running-account Run and RunOnce registration names, Startup folder file names, Windows startup approval states, and boot or logon scheduled-task names and states, then return one startup-environment conclusion. Command lines, target paths, binary approval timestamps, task actions, arguments, accounts and credentials are not collected. Registration does not prove an item ran, caused a delay, is unwanted or is safe to disable. Approval state does not prove a matching registration exists. No registration, file, application, task, service or policy change is selected. Microsoft app removal Review selected OneDrive, Outlook, Office/M365, Project and Teams registrations with account-scope and retention choices. Machine, running-account and registered-package inventories are correlated; all-user review also includes packages provisioned for new users. A supported current-account package can receive a separate exact safeguard plan in the desktop or CLI. Removing that one package is a separate guarded desktop action with its own confirmations. The review itself changes no process, application, package, file, credential, folder location, registry value or user data. Outlook reset Review New Outlook, Classic Outlook or both with explicit choices for caches, profile settings, authentication, OST files, add-ins and New Outlook reinstall. Running-account review checks bounded known locations, exact registrations and closed-process readiness. A separately confirmed cache-only action retains exact cache directories under unique recovery names and verifies every move. All-user review and other reset areas remain review-only. No retained cache, profile, OST file, credential, add-in, package or setting is removed. Foxit inventory Inventory machine-installed Foxit products and updater registry values in both views. User installations, effective policy and product ownership are not verified. No applications or settings are changed. Windows Installer service Check Installer restriction, rollback, elevation, logging and debug policy, in-progress markers, engine binaries and registered cached-package visibility before service recovery. Correlate recent failed and successful outcomes only for exact reported product identities. If prerequisites pass, auto-repair can start the enabled service and verify Running. Historical outcomes, logging, cache visibility and debug findings do not authorize package or policy changes. Policies, logs, transaction markers, startup types and cached packages are preserved. A specific package installation is not tested. Windows Search Check Windows Search service availability, current SystemIndex activity and recent Search event history. Auto-repair can start a stopped, enabled service and verify running state. A separate explicit index-rebuild choice retains the prior index, starts a fresh index and verifies exact service and directory state. Indexed locations, policy, startup type and retained data are preserved. Starting the service or rebuilding the index can resume background indexing. Search result completeness remains untested. App Readiness Check App Readiness service availability. Optional recovery starts only the enabled, stopped exact service after fresh dependency checks. Existing first-sign-in and application provisioning may resume under current policy. Packages, registrations, licenses, user profiles, application data, policy and startup type are preserved. KillerBench selects no package operation. Verification covers stable service state only, not sign-in, provisioning or application launch. State Repository Service Check State Repository Service availability. Optional recovery starts only the enabled, stopped exact service after fresh dependency checks. Existing application-model state requests may resume. Registrations, packages, licenses, profiles, browser and application data, repository content, policy and startup type are preserved. No reset, re-registration or deletion is requested. Verification covers stable service state only, not repository consistency or application operation. AppX Deployment Service Check AppX Deployment Service availability. Optional recovery starts only the enabled, stopped exact service after fresh dependency checks. Existing deployment, registration or servicing requests may resume under current policy. Packages, registrations, licenses, profiles, application data, repository cont"},{"u":"help.html","a":"maintenance","g":"Help","t":"Maintenance","h":[["maintenance","Maintenance"],["maintenance_tools-in-this-section","Tools in this section"],["maintenance_cleanup","Cleanup"],["maintenance_profiles","Profiles"],["maintenance_scheduled-tasks","Scheduled tasks"],["maintenance_declutter-windows","Declutter Windows"]],"x":"Maintenance Maintenance keeps a machine tidy: file cleanup, old user profiles, scheduled tasks and decluttering Windows. Every tool previews what it would remove before anything is deleted. Step-by-step guides: Free up disk space and Make Windows more private . Tools in this section Clean up files, profiles and scheduled tasks, and declutter Windows. Cleanup Estimate selected temporary-file, cache and retained diagnostic-evidence targets using the age and file-limit settings in the main panel. Windows Temp and Windows SystemTemp are independent targets. Delivery Optimization is a separate cache choice and removed content may need to be downloaded again. Optional permanent removal uses the separate cleanup checkbox, rechecks retained candidates within the selected limit and records each outcome. Deletion bypasses the Recycle Bin. Targets, age and file limits are also configurable through the cleanup CLI command. Profiles Review Windows profiles using the selected age threshold, exact ProfileList registrations and optional service and scheduled-task account dependencies, then return one bounded review conclusion. Loaded, system and current-account profiles are excluded from review eligibility. Candidates remain review-only. No profile, folder, registry key, account, service, task, credential, policy or file is changed, and no removal is selected. Application dependencies and backup recoverability remain unverified. Scheduled tasks Inventory visible scheduled tasks, states and reported principals. No tasks are started, registered or changed. Actions, triggers and execution success are not verified. Declutter Windows Review explicit Windows cleanup areas such as Recall and AI, telemetry, search suggestions, consumer content, taskbar and Start, notifications, Edge, power, hibernation, Office languages and preinstalled software for the running account or all profiles. Review is read-only. High performance power, sleep and display timers, and hibernation can each be changed when that area is the only selection, with fresh evidence and explicit confirmation."},{"u":"help.html","a":"onboarding","g":"Help","t":"Onboarding","h":[["onboarding","Onboarding"],["onboarding_checking-an-installer-before-you-run-it","Checking an installer before you run it"],["onboarding_tools-in-this-section","Tools in this section"],["onboarding_onboarding-2","Onboarding"],["onboarding_computer-name","Computer name"]],"x":"Onboarding Onboarding prepares a new computer. It reviews the applications and configuration you select and names the computer. It does not download or install anything by itself. Step-by-step guides: Set up a new computer . Checking an installer before you run it The command line can verify a vendor installer before you run it: KillerBench trusts an installer only when it carries a valid Authenticode signature from the vendor's exact organization name. It never runs the installer. Tools in this section Prepare a new computer: applications, name and configuration. Onboarding Review explicitly selected provisioning applications and configuration groups. Current uninstall, app-package and Office Click-to-Run registrations are correlated without downloading or executing installers. Configuration selections describe consequences and route to separately guarded workflows when implemented. No download, installation, account, update, cleanup, policy, registry, service, profile or restart action occurs. Computer name Review the proposed computer name against current identity and domain role. No rename, credential collection or restart. Name availability, permissions and application dependencies remain unverified."},{"u":"help.html","a":"security","g":"Help","t":"Security","h":[["security","Security"],["security_tools-in-this-section","Tools in this section"],["security_microsoft-defender","Microsoft Defender"],["security_windows-security-center","Windows Security Center"],["security_firewall-services","Firewall services"],["security_certificate-expiration","Certificate expiration"],["security_credential-manager","Credential Manager"],["security_web-account-manager","Web Account Manager"],["security_microsoft-account-sign-in-assistant","Microsoft Account Sign-in Assistant"]],"x":"Security Security inspects Microsoft Defender, the firewall, certificates and the sign-in services. It also exposes the TPM and platform security state that BitLocker and Windows Hello depend on. Step-by-step guides: Encrypt a drive with BitLocker and Make Windows more private . Tools in this section Inspect protection, firewall, certificates and sign-in services. Microsoft Defender Inspect Defender protection and signature status. Auto-repair refreshes signatures only when Defender reports active protection and outdated signatures. Uses configured signature sources. No protection, exclusion or policy changes; no malware scan. Windows Security Center Review bounded antivirus registrations with raw provider state, exact service-failure history and current Windows Security Center readiness without interpreting protection health. An empty inventory is not a protection-health verdict, and registration count, raw state or historical failures cannot authorize recovery or a security change. Optional recovery starts only the enabled, stopped exact service after fresh dependency checks. Existing security status monitoring and notifications may resume. Antivirus configuration, provider registrations, protection settings, exclusions, notifications, policy and startup type are preserved. No scan, update or protection change is requested. Verification covers stable service state only, not antivirus presence, protection state, signature freshness, notification delivery, registration ownership or effective policy. Firewall services Read Domain, Private and Public profile enabled settings from ActiveStore, then check Base Filtering Engine and Windows Defender Firewall services. Optional recovery starts only enabled, stopped services after fresh dependency checks. Existing filtering policy may take effect and interrupt network or remote access. Startup types, service permissions, firewall rules, profiles and IPsec settings are preserved. No resets, rule changes or service stops. Verification establishes service running state only; effective protection and connectivity remain unverified. Certificate expiration Inspect certificate dates and build cache-only local trust chains for the machine and current-user personal stores, then return one bounded operational conclusion. Revocation, server presentation, bindings, enrollment, policy ownership, remote trust and private-key usability remain unverified. No certificate, trust setting, enrollment, binding, private key, application, service or policy is changed. Credential Manager Check Credential Manager service availability. Optional recovery starts only the enabled, stopped exact service after fresh dependency checks. Existing applications may resume requests for stored credentials. Vault contents, passwords, passkeys, certificates, tokens, credential registrations, permissions, policy, service permissions and startup type are preserved. No credential content or operation is requested. Verification covers stable service state only, not credential retrieval, authentication or application access. Web Account Manager Review payload-sanitized account broker history and exact Web Account Manager availability. Optional recovery starts only the enabled, stopped exact service after fresh dependency checks. Existing applications may resume account and token requests under current policy. Historical events cannot authorize recovery or establish current account state. Accounts, credentials, tokens, cookies, application registrations, permissions, sign-in state, policy, service permissions and startup type are preserved. KillerBench selects no account, credential, token, cookie, permission or sign-in operation. Verification covers stable service state only, not authentication, token issuance or application access. Microsoft Account Sign-in Assistant Review payload-sanitized account broker history and exact Microsoft Account Sign-in Assistant availability. Optional recovery starts only the enabled, stopped exact service after fresh dependency checks. Existing Microsoft account sign-in requests may resume under current policy. Historical events cannot authorize recovery or establish current account state. Accounts, credentials, tokens, device registration, application data, permissions, sign-in state, policy, service permissions and startup type are preserved. KillerBench selects no account, credential, token, registration, permission or sign-in operation. Verification covers stable service state only, not authentication, account connectivity or application access."},{"u":"help.html","a":"bitlocker","g":"Help","t":"BitLocker","h":[["bitlocker","BitLocker"],["bitlocker_using-the-bitlocker-tool","Using the BitLocker tool"],["bitlocker_more-bitlocker-actions","More BitLocker actions"],["bitlocker_tpm-and-fips","TPM and FIPS"],["bitlocker_tools-in-this-section","Tools in this section"],["bitlocker_bitlocker-2","BitLocker"]],"x":"BitLocker BitLocker inspects drive encryption, protectors and recovery information, then helps you back up, protect or change a drive. It works on this computer only. Step-by-step guides: Encrypt a drive with BitLocker . Using the BitLocker tool Choose the drive letter and an Operation , then press Run diagnosis . - Inspect reads encryption state, protection status and protector identities. It is the default and changes nothing. - Back up recovery writes the selected recovery password to a new file outside the protected drive and verifies it. Press Find recovery protectors first, choose the protector, then confirm the backup. - Encrypt backs up the selected recovery password, verifies the backup, then starts encryption once with the method you choose. The Windows drive needs a TPM protector and a recovery password. A data drive needs only a recovery password. - Resume protection turns protection back on for a fully encrypted drive where it was suspended. Each of these needs its own confirmation box, and KillerBench rechecks the drive immediately before it acts. More BitLocker actions Open More BitLocker actions for changes that sit outside Run diagnosis . Choose an action, press Preview to read exactly what it requires, then confirm and press Run action . - Turn on auto-unlock makes an encrypted data drive unlock when Windows starts. The Windows drive must be fully encrypted with protection on. - Add a TPM protector or Add a recovery password creates one new protector. KillerBench never shows or logs a new recovery password, so back it up straight away. - Back up the recovery password to Active Directory or Microsoft Entra ID sends the protector you selected above. KillerBench reports that Windows accepted the request. It cannot read the directory copy, so confirm it there. - Decrypt this drive starts decryption, for example before encrypting again with a required method. It needs a second confirmation because the drive is unprotected afterwards, and it refuses the Windows drive while other drives auto-unlock from it. TPM and FIPS If the TPM is present but not ready, the Security section can prepare it without clearing it. KillerBench never clears a TPM, because that destroys keys held by BitLocker and Windows Hello. The FIPS algorithm policy switch is available as an explicit, reversible repair for organizations that need FIPS-approved BitLocker PINs. Tools in this section Inspect encryption, protectors and recovery information, then back them up or protect the drive. BitLocker Preview BitLocker options and inspect the selected volume. No encryption or recovery backup is performed by this workflow."},{"u":"help.html","a":"privacy","g":"Help","t":"Privacy","h":[["privacy","Privacy"],["privacy_reviewing-and-changing-settings","Reviewing and changing settings"],["privacy_tools-in-this-section","Tools in this section"],["privacy_privacy-2","Privacy"]],"x":"Privacy Privacy reads the privacy settings for this machine and the account running KillerBench, then lets you change the ones you choose. It goes beyond the Windows Settings app, covering app permissions, cloud search and search highlights. Step-by-step guides: Make Windows more private . Reviewing and changing settings Select the privacy areas to inspect and press Run diagnosis . Every area starts unchecked and the review is read-only. To change a setting, use the Privacy setting control. KillerBench checks whether a policy or device management already owns the setting, records the current value, makes the one change you asked for and reads it back to confirm it. Settings owned by policy are left alone. The settings include diagnostic data and advertising choices, clipboard history, 17 app-permission settings such as camera, microphone and location, cloud content search for personal and work accounts, and search highlights. Tools in this section Review and change app permissions, suggestions, search and advertising settings. Privacy Read selected privacy registry settings for this machine and the account running KillerBench. Separate explicit controls can change Clipboard history or a supported account setting after fresh policy checks, durable preparation and exact verification. Other privacy selections remain read-only. Registry snapshots do not prove effective permissions or policy ownership."},{"u":"help.html","a":"storage","g":"Help","t":"Storage","h":[["storage","Storage"],["storage_tools-in-this-section","Tools in this section"],["storage_storage-2","Storage"],["storage_storage-spaces","Storage Spaces"],["storage_storage-service","Storage Service"],["storage_optimize-drives-service","Optimize Drives service"]],"x":"Storage Inspect disks, volumes, Storage Spaces and drive maintenance services. Diagnosis does not select a disk operation; any supported machine-changing action needs its own exact target, prerequisites and confirmation. Step-by-step guides: Check and repair a drive and Free up disk space . Tools in this section Storage Map bounded disk, partition and drive-letter volume identities, evaluate 10% free-space pressure across every drive-letter volume, inspect VSS shadow-storage allocation and per-volume quota defaults, summarize current Storage module job progress, check the selected NTFS volume and its online self-healing state, and inspect disk/controller devices across this PC. Capacity thresholds, quota settings and job states are review evidence and do not select cleanup, resizing, quota changes or job control. Drive-letter mapping is point-in-time and does not cover every mount path. Device findings are not mapped to the selected volume. The Windows volume is the default. No files, snapshots, jobs or drivers are changed, no setting is changed, no volume is dismounted, and no restart is scheduled. Storage Spaces Inventory non-primordial Storage Spaces pools and virtual disks, including health, operational state, capacity, allocation and resiliency, then return one bounded operational conclusion. Windows may demand-start its Storage Spaces management provider while answering this read-only query. Pools, virtual disks, physical disks, volumes, resiliency, allocation, maintenance settings, policy and startup type are preserved. No pool, disk, volume, allocation, attachment, repair, optimization or setting operation is requested. Provider results do not verify pool accessibility, redundancy, data integrity, backup recoverability or application I/O. Storage Service Review bounded current Storage module jobs, exact service-failure history and current Storage Service readiness. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. Job state and historical failures cannot authorize service recovery or storage changes. Optional recovery starts only the enabled, stopped exact service after fresh dependency checks. Existing storage notifications and policy-managed storage activity may resume. Volumes, partitions, filesystems, mount points, devices, drivers, Storage Sense settings, maintenance policy, service permissions and startup type are preserved. KillerBench selects no job, volume, partition, mount point, file, device, driver or storage setting operation. Verification covers stable service state only, not job outcome, device, volume or storage-policy operation. Optimize Drives service Check Optimize Drives service availability. Optional recovery starts only the enabled, stopped exact service after fresh dependency checks. Existing Windows-scheduled optimization activity may resume under current maintenance policy. Volumes, filesystems, data, optimization schedules, maintenance policy, service permissions and startup type are preserved. KillerBench does not select a volume, analyze, trim, defragment, optimize or schedule any drive. Verification covers stable service state only, not analysis, optimization or device health."},{"u":"help.html","a":"reports","g":"Help","t":"Reports and evidence","h":[["reports","Reports and evidence"],["reports_states-you-will-see","States you will see"],["reports_raw-output-and-the-readable-report","Raw output and the readable report"],["reports_saved-logs","Saved logs"],["reports_exporting","Exporting"]],"x":"Reports and evidence Every run produces a report you can read, filter and export. The report always separates what was proven from what was only observed. States you will see State Meaning --- --- Needs review The evidence supports a problem that deserves attention. Information Evidence was collected and is shown for context. Incomplete or Unknown A check could not finish or the evidence was unavailable. It is never counted as healthy. Historical An event from the past. It explains history but does not prove a current fault. Verified A repair or change was confirmed by reading the result back. Raw output and the readable report Raw output keeps the technical evidence with original line numbers so you can trace any finding back to its source. The Readable report summarizes the same run in plain language, with the next safe step first. Saved logs KillerBench can also read saved Windows logs, such as CBS and Setup logs, without running anything on the machine. Findings link to the exact line that produced them. Load sample in Session sources opens a synthetic example you can explore safely. Exporting Export report saves the report with its source paths, parsed entries and evidence excerpts, for review before attaching it to a ticket. Raw logs and evidence excerpts can contain computer names, accounts, paths or sensitive source text. Guarded collectors restrict what they retain, but imported evidence is not guaranteed to be redacted. Inspect and remove sensitive information before sharing."},{"u":"help.html","a":"cli","g":"Help","t":"Command line","h":[["cli","Command line"],["cli_updates-and-servicing","Updates and servicing"],["cli_bitlocker-and-tpm","BitLocker and TPM"],["cli_security-and-privacy","Security and privacy"],["cli_services","Services"],["cli_storage-files-and-backup","Storage, files and backup"],["cli_printing","Printing"],["cli_network","Network"],["cli_apps","Apps"],["cli_system","System"],["cli_knowledge-validation-and-engine","Knowledge, validation and engine"],["cli_other-commands","Other commands"]],"x":"Command line killerbench-cli runs the same Engine as the desktop app from PowerShell or a remote console. Every command that could change the machine previews by default and needs an explicit flag such as --execute before it acts. Commands print JSON evidence to standard output and progress to standard error. Step-by-step guides: Run KillerBench from scripts . Exit code 0 means the preview or verified result succeeded, 1 means a startup, input or journal error, 2 means the arguments were not recognized, 3 means blocked, failed or unverified, 4 means a workflow needs a restart before it can verify, and 130 means you stopped it. Run killerbench-cli with no arguments to print this list on your own machine. Updates and servicing - bits-job-review JOB_GUID --all-users --observe : Exact transfer review or two-snapshot observation; no retry. - bits-jobs --all-users : BITS job states, owners and byte counts; no transfer changes. - bits-retry JOB_GUID --execute : Preview by default; explicit exact-job retry, no application-completion claim. - bits-retry-recover JOB_GUID : Two read-only snapshots; archives stable outcomes without another retry. - component-repair-source : Managed DISM repair-source policy and conflicts; no changes. - delivery-optimization : Read-only Delivery Optimization service state; JSON evidence. - delivery-optimization-activity : Privacy-bounded current job counts and byte totals; no transfer identities or changes. - delivery-optimization-policy : Read-only classic and Policy CSP settings; sensitive values and provider identities excluded. - feature-service-events : Read exact Time, Search, WMI, Event Log and audio service failure history. - feature-update-policy : Read-only target release, deferral, pause and safeguard policy; JSON evidence. - pending-file-operations : Read-only privacy-bounded pending file operation summary; JSON evidence. - restart-check : Read-only pending restart sources; JSON evidence. - servicing-logs : Bounded newest CBS, plain-text and compressed CBS archives, DISM and Windows Setup log analysis; no repair. - servicing-monitor --refresh 1..60 --samples 1..120 --skip-events --skip-history --skip-services --skip-restart --skip-setup --skip-compatibility --skip-readiness --skip-transfers --skip-delivery-optimization --skip-installer --skip-scheduled-task : Read-only Servicing activity snapshots. - servicing-service-events : Read-only exact Windows Update servicing-chain failure histories. - servicing-service-states : Read-only current state for the six-service Windows Update servicing chain. - setup-code DECIMAL_OR_0xHEX : Documented Windows Setup result-code reference; no machine inspection or repair. - update-applicability : Read-only Windows edition, build and architecture evidence. - update-cache : Bounded read-only SoftwareDistribution cache totals; no filenames. - update-code DECIMAL_OR_0xHEX : Documented Windows Update result-code reference; no machine inspection or repair. - update-context : Read-only current elevation and desktop-session evidence; no account identifiers. - update-engine-files : Read-only native Windows Update engine presence, version and signature evidence. - update-events : Read-only Windows Update Client history from the last seven days; JSON including raw evidence. - update-followup DECIMAL_OR_0xHEX : Fresh read-only diagnostics selected by a documented Windows Update result code. - update-history : Read-only newest 100 Windows Update operation outcomes; JSON evidence. - update-plan --select GUID:REVISION --drivers --no-software --allow-restart-required --json --execute : Repeat --select ; preview by default. - update-policy : Read-only managed Windows Update policy; JSON evidence. - update-policy-surfaces : Read-only classic and Policy CSP configuration-surface evidence; values and provider identities excluded. - update-recover : Check a pending update; never installs or retries it. - update-restart-policy : Read-only compliance deadlines, grace periods, restart suppression and notification behavior. - update-search --drivers --no-software --filter TITLE --downloaded-only --classification GUID --product GUID : JSON discovery and matches; no installation. - update-service-events : Read-only exact Windows Update service failure history. - update-service-files : Read-only exact Windows Update service DLL registration and signature evidence. - update-services : Read-only Windows Update service registrations; JSON evidence. - update-store-reset --source Configured WindowsUpdate MicrosoftUpdate --drivers --no-software --execute : Fresh-search gated; preview by default; retains the old store. - update-store-reset-recover : Reconcile a prepared store reset without repeating it. - windows-capabilities : Read-only online Windows capability states; JSON result. - windows-update-task : Read-only exact Scheduled Start task state and runtime history; no task execution or update operation. BitLocker and TPM - bitlocker-auto-unlock DRIVE: --execute : Preview by default; turns on auto-unlock for an encrypted data volume. - bitlocker-auto-unlock-recover : Verify an interrupted auto-unlock request without repeating it. - bitlocker-backup DRIVE: PROTECTOR_GUID FILE_PATH --execute : Preview by default; new recovery backup file only. - bitlocker-backup-recover DRIVE: PROTECTOR_GUID FILE_PATH : Verify an interrupted backup without writing it again. - bitlocker-decrypt DRIVE: --execute --confirm-unprotected : Preview by default; starts decryption, for example before re-encrypting with a required method. - bitlocker-decrypt-recover : Verify an interrupted decryption request without repeating it. - bitlocker-encrypt DRIVE: RECOVERY_PROTECTOR_GUID NEW_BACKUP_FILE --encryption XtsAes128 --used-space-only --execute : Preview by default; requires existing TPM and recovery protectors. - bitlocker-encrypt-recover : Verify pending conversion state without repeating the request. - bitlocker-escrow DRIVE: RECOVERY_PROTECTOR_GUID ad entra --execute : Preview"},{"u":"help.html","a":"troubleshooting","g":"Help","t":"Troubleshooting","h":[["troubleshooting","Troubleshooting"],["troubleshooting_an-action-says-it-is-blocked","An action says it is blocked"],["troubleshooting_a-previous-request-needs-reconciliation","A previous request needs reconciliation"],["troubleshooting_a-check-shows-incomplete-or-unknown","A check shows Incomplete or Unknown"],["troubleshooting_where-files-are-kept","Where files are kept"],["troubleshooting_reporting-a-problem","Reporting a problem"]],"x":"Troubleshooting An action says it is blocked A blocked result means the requested action could not proceed. Read the message and journal to see whether it stopped before dispatch or after an earlier step. Do not assume a partially completed operation was rolled back. The most common prerequisite reasons are below. - Administrator access is required. Close KillerBench and start it again with Run as administrator . - Restart readiness must be clear. Windows has a restart pending. Restart the computer, then run the action again. - Managed policy is present. Group policy or device management owns the setting. Change it there instead. - Evidence changed. Something changed between the first check and the final recheck. Run the tool again to read the new state. A previous request needs reconciliation KillerBench found a pending record from an interrupted request. Run the same action again. It checks what happened and reports the real outcome without repeating the request. On the command line, use the matching -recover command, for example killerbench-cli bitlocker-auto-unlock-recover . A check shows Incomplete or Unknown The evidence could not be read, often because KillerBench was not running as administrator or a Windows service was unavailable. KillerBench reports this honestly instead of assuming the computer is healthy. Where files are kept Journals, pending records and saved evidence live under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\KillerBench . Each guarded action keeps its own folder. Keep them when you need a record of what changed. Reporting a problem KillerBench is in development. Include the exported report and the activity log when you report a problem, and remove anything you would not share."},{"u":"technical.html","a":"tech-stack","g":"Technical","t":"Tech stack","h":[["tech-stack","Tech stack"]],"x":"Tech stack KillerBench is a native Windows app. The desktop app, the command line and the Engine underneath them share the same diagnostic evidence and repair workflows. Component Detail UI WPF on .NET 10 ( net10.0-windows ), Windows x64, custom window chrome Engine Engine.Core , Engine and Engine.Windows , with no reference to WPF or desktop controls Command line killerbench-cli , a separate host on the same Engine Windows providers .NET, Windows APIs, PowerShell and Windows command-line tools, each run behind fixed time, record and size bounds Reports Raw output, Readable report, HTML, text and JSON Reference data Versioned knowledge packs for error codes, hardware identifiers, vendors and support references Packaging Installer: framework-dependent, needs .NET Desktop Runtime 10 for Windows x64. Portable: self-contained, no runtime install Appearance 13 family themes and 20 languages"},{"u":"technical.html","a":"install-folders-and-data","g":"Technical","t":"Install, folders and data","h":[["install-folders-and-data","Install, folders and data"],["what-the-installer-does","What the installer does"],["where-things-live","Where things live"]],"x":"Install, folders and data KillerBench ships in two forms. The installer is the standard download and supports a per-user or an all-users installation. The portable edition carries its own .NET runtime and runs without installation. KillerBench runs with the rights of the account that started it. It does not elevate itself. Start it with Run as administrator when protected evidence, such as BitLocker protectors, or a repair needs administrator access. Elevation never selects or approves a repair. What the installer does The installer verifies and extracts the application to %LOCALAPPDATA%\\Programs\\KillerBench\\ , or to %ProgramFiles%\\KillerBench\\ after an elevation prompt for all users. It adds a Start menu shortcut, an optional desktop shortcut and an Add or Remove Programs entry with a normal and a quiet uninstall. The portable edition verifies and extracts its payload under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\KillerBench\\Portable\\ for the current run and sweeps abandoned copies on the next start. Where things live Location Holds %LOCALAPPDATA%\\KillerBench\\appearance.json Theme, accent, interface scale and language %LOCALAPPDATA%\\KillerBench\\Workflows\\ One JSON journal per workflow run: decisions, bounded command output and repair results %LOCALAPPDATA%\\KillerBench\\Repairs\\ Execution journals for servicing repairs such as DISM and SFC Feature folders under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\KillerBench\\ Pending guards and outcomes for individual guarded actions, for example BitLockerBackup , Time , Firewall and Profiles %LOCALAPPDATA%\\KillerBench\\agent-repairs.json The KillerMCP repair level chosen in About HKCU or HKLM\\Software\\KillerBench The installed path, for the matching installation scope A .kbench file you choose A saved capture with its retained evidence Nothing is uploaded. Delete a journal only after you no longer need the evidence for an uncertain outcome."},{"u":"technical.html","a":"diagnose-first","g":"Technical","t":"Diagnose first","h":[["diagnose-first","Diagnose first"],["bounded-collection","Bounded collection"],["minimal-retention","Minimal retention"]],"x":"Diagnose first KillerBench treats diagnosis and change as different jobs. Diagnosis only reads. A change happens only when you pick a specific repair or action, review its options and prerequisites, and confirm it. The full system scan is diagnostic-only, including when a focused workflow has automatic repair enabled. Saved captures, imported logs and historical events never authorize a repair. Bounded collection Every native command and Windows provider runs behind fixed time, record and size bounds. A provider records its exit status, timeout, truncation, standard output and error output. A failed or truncated provider can never be read as a clean, empty answer. Minimal retention Collectors keep what a finding needs. Many results keep names, categories, counts, states and timestamps while leaving out credentials, command lines, full paths, account identities, network endpoints and unrelated event payloads."},{"u":"technical.html","a":"evidence-you-can-follow","g":"Technical","t":"Evidence you can follow","h":[["evidence-you-can-follow","Evidence you can follow"],["unknown-is-a-result","Unknown is a result"],["history-stays-history","History stays history"]],"x":"Evidence you can follow Every finding is tied to the evidence that produced it, and the report always separates what was proven from what was only observed. State Meaning Needs review The evidence supports a problem that deserves attention. Information Evidence was collected and is shown for context. Incomplete or Unknown A check could not finish or the evidence was unavailable. It is never counted as healthy. Historical An event from the past. It explains history but does not prove a current fault. Verified A repair or change was confirmed by reading the result back. Unknown is a result Unknown means access was denied, a source was missing, a provider failed, output was malformed, a bound was exceeded, or the evidence disagreed. KillerBench shows that limitation and the next useful step instead of hiding it. History stays history Event records and imported logs describe what was recorded at a past time. They can point to an error code or the next diagnostic scope. They do not prove that the fault is still active."},{"u":"technical.html","a":"guarded-repairs","g":"Technical","t":"Guarded repairs","h":[["guarded-repairs","Guarded repairs"],["explicit-choices","Explicit choices"],["managed-computers","Managed computers"],["automatic-repair","Automatic repair"]],"x":"Guarded repairs Repairs are typed operations with eligibility, execution, verification and recovery rules. They are not command strings picked out of a log line. Every supported repair follows the same sequence: Collect complete current evidence. Match one supported recipe. Review required options and consequences. Confirm administrative and policy prerequisites. Recollect the decisive evidence immediately before the change. Write a durable preparation record. Run one bounded native action. Collect fresh verification evidence. Record a verified, blocked, stopped or uncertain outcome. If the decisive evidence changes before the action runs, KillerBench stops without performing it. Explicit choices Repairs that depend on an option need an exact choice from you, such as a volume, printer queue, recovery-key destination, power value, package version or repair source. KillerBench never invents those values. Managed computers Group Policy and device management can make a local change ineffective or inappropriate. Supported repairs inspect who owns a setting and stop when that ownership is managed or unknown. Settings that a policy owns are left alone. Automatic repair Automatic repair only lets eligible recipes proceed. It does not bypass evidence, choose missing options, ignore policy, retry an uncertain action, restart Windows, or turn a finding that needs review into a repair."},{"u":"technical.html","a":"journals-and-interruptions","g":"Technical","t":"Journals and interruptions","h":[["journals-and-interruptions","Journals and interruptions"]],"x":"Journals and interruptions Before KillerBench asks Windows to make a change, it writes down what it is about to do. Each change is saved in a journal under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\KillerBench . After the change, KillerBench reads the result back and reports Verified only when Windows shows the expected state. If Windows reports success but the state did not change, the result says so and nothing is repeated. A process can stop after Windows receives a request but before verification finishes. The pending record stays, and a later reconciliation reads fresh state to work out what happened. Reconciliation is not an automatic rollback, and it never repeats the original change. Keep the journal when an outcome is uncertain."},{"u":"technical.html","a":"constants-and-limits","g":"Technical","t":"Constants and limits","h":[["constants-and-limits","Constants and limits"]],"x":"Constants and limits Specification Value Diagnostic coverage Full system scan runs 49 diagnostic areas and checks 96 guarded repair recipes Text source read 1,000,000 characters per source, 2,000 matching lines shown Session sources 12 in the sidebar; a capture holds up to 24 sources and 64 MB Event queries Newest 200 events per channel from the last 7 days Windows Update scan 15 seconds per source, two minutes overall Log investigation 10,000 parsed entries, 256 distinct codes, 8 excerpts of 320 characters per code Compressed CBS archive 256 MiB archive, 512 MiB log member, 30 second extraction Read-only CHKDSK Ten minutes; a repair is bounded to 65 minutes DNS and TCP checks Ten seconds by default, one minute at most Service inventory Up to 1,024 installed services Knowledge packs Up to 32 open at once Large file review Files of at least 100 MB Servicing activity monitor 1 to 60 second interval, 1 to 120 snapshots Scheduled task drafts Execution limit of 1 to 4,320 minutes Custom time sources Up to eight NTP peers Activity export Up to 150 retained entries Languages and themes 20 languages, 13 themes with accent variants"},{"u":"technical.html","a":"what-it-will-not-do","g":"Technical","t":"What it will not do","h":[["what-it-will-not-do","What it will not do"]],"x":"What it will not do Clear a TPM, delete protectors it did not create, or display recovery passwords. Change a setting that group policy or device management controls. Run a repair just because a historical event or imported report mentions a problem. Turn missing, partial or failed evidence into a healthy verdict."},{"u":"technical.html","a":"reports","g":"Technical","t":"Reports","h":[["reports","Reports"]],"x":"Reports Every run produces a report that you can read, filter and export. A report separates: What was inspected What problem was found What evidence supports the finding What action was proposed or performed How the result was verified What remains unknown or needs a restart Raw output keeps the technical evidence with its original line numbers so you can trace any finding to its source. The Readable report summarizes the same run in plain language, with the next safe step first. HTML reports use the Killer Tools theme system and stay readable without the app. Text favors portability, and JSON keeps structured fields for another program."},{"u":"technical.html","a":"saved-logs-and-captures","g":"Technical","t":"Saved logs and captures","h":[["saved-logs-and-captures","Saved logs and captures"]],"x":"Saved logs and captures KillerBench can investigate evidence without running anything on the machine. Drop a text log on the window, or paste its path and choose Open log . CBS, CSI and Setup lines are parsed with their timestamps, and findings link to the exact original line. EVTX and event XML files can be imported the same way. Scan Windows Update is an on-demand, read-only collection of CBS, DISM, Panther and rollback Setup logs, update and servicing event channels, update service states, free space, uptime, pending restart indicators and selected update policy. Save capture writes the retained evidence to a .kbench file. Open capture reopens it without the original files and evaluates it again with the installed rules, so the same rules give the same findings."},{"u":"technical.html","a":"demo-mode","g":"Technical","t":"Demo mode","h":[["demo-mode","Demo mode"]],"x":"Demo mode Start KillerBench.exe --demo to try Run diagnosis on a fabricated workstation. Every workflow produces synthetic evidence and timed progress. No Windows provider is called, no repair command runs and no journal is written. Separate tool buttons outside Run diagnosis stay live, so do not use them as demo actions. KillerBench.exe --demo-screenshot opens a view-only Full system scan report for a fixed fictional machine. Clicks in the workspace, shortcuts and file drops are blocked."},{"u":"technical.html","a":"knowledge-packs","g":"Technical","t":"Knowledge packs","h":[["knowledge-packs","Knowledge packs"]],"x":"Knowledge packs Large and frequently updated reference data lives in knowledge packs instead of the diagnostic logic: Windows and Setup error codes, vendor and device identifiers, PCI, USB, ACPI and UEFI references, network OUI vendor data, driver and support locations, and curated support links. Each pack identifies its format, schema version, source, source revision, generation time and content hash, and KillerBench validates them before use. An unknown or invalid pack stays unavailable instead of falling back to a guess. An update downloads to a temporary location, is validated, then replaces the active pack in one step, so a failed update leaves the previous pack in place. Knowledge data can explain an identifier or suggest where to look next. It cannot turn a historical code into a current fault, choose a driver automatically, or authorize a repair."},{"u":"technical.html","a":"updates-and-signing","g":"Technical","t":"Updates and signing","h":[["updates-and-signing","Updates and signing"]],"x":"Updates and signing At startup KillerBench asks GitHub for the latest stable release, with a four second limit, and offers an update only when that release is newer. You can turn the check off with Check for updates on startup in About, or in the update prompt. The download is the package that matches the running copy, installer or portable, and its SHA-256 must match the release's SHA256SUMS.txt before anything installs. The app closes and reopens itself to finish. There is no background service. The About card shows the version, the code-signing publisher and thumbprint, and the SHA-256 of the running executable, so you can check the copy you have."},{"u":"technical.html","a":"killermcp","g":"Technical","t":"KillerMCP","h":[["killermcp","KillerMCP"]],"x":"KillerMCP KillerBench does not include an AI agent. KillerMCP can connect one to the same Engine. The KillerMCP Options in About decide what that agent may do: Setting Effect Off (default) The agent cannot request a repair. Allow safe repairs The agent can request repairs that are classified as safe. Also allow disruptive repairs Disruptive repairs become available, and each still needs approval. Only a person at this computer can raise the level. After an uncertain repair result, KillerMCP writes a lock, and agent repairs stay blocked until someone clears it in About."},{"u":"technical.html","a":"localization","g":"Technical","t":"Localization","h":[["localization","Localization"]],"x":"Localization The interface is available in 20 languages: English, Bengali, Czech, German, Spanish, French, Hungarian, Italian, Japanese, Kazakh, Norwegian, Polish, Portuguese (Brazil), Russian, Slovenian, Turkish, Ukrainian, Vietnamese, and Simplified and Traditional Chinese. A language change applies immediately, including the section and workflow names, the welcome text and an open readable report. Any missing text falls back to English. Evidence, Engine findings, journals and Raw output stay in English, the same as a Windows log, so a report reads the same on every machine and can be searched for exact text."},{"u":"technical.html","a":"command-line-and-exit-codes","g":"Technical","t":"Command line and exit codes","h":[["command-line-and-exit-codes","Command line and exit codes"]],"x":"Command line and exit codes killerbench-cli runs the same Engine without the desktop app. Progress goes to standard error and JSON to standard output. Most commands preview by default, and a change needs --execute . Matching -recover commands review an interrupted action without repeating it. See Run KillerBench from scripts and the command reference . Exit code Workflow commands Scan and open 0 No unresolved issue in the checked scopes Completed coverage 1 Startup or journal error Failure 2 Invalid arguments Invalid arguments 3 Attention required Partial collection 4 Restart required before verification 130 Stopped Canceled or overall timeout No exit code certifies that a computer is healthy."},{"u":"technical.html","a":"architecture","g":"Technical","t":"Architecture","h":[["architecture","Architecture"]],"x":"Architecture The Engine is split into three UI-free projects. Engine.Core supplies primitives and declarative recipes, Engine owns shared workflows and evidence, and Engine.Windows registers the native providers. The desktop app, the CLI and the tests are separate hosts. Layer Responsibility Capability collectors Read bounded Windows state for one exact scope Evidence parsers Validate shape, identity, completeness and limits Diagnostic interpretation Convert evidence into explicit workflow states Workflow orchestration Combine checks, progress, recipes and journals Repair coordinators Recheck eligibility, prepare, change, verify and reconcile Reports Present problems, actions, results and limitations Knowledge packs Resolve versioned reference data without changing logic Collectors fail closed when identity is ambiguous, output is malformed, a limit is exceeded, or required authority cannot be inspected. The Engine targets Windows today and is not cross-platform. The Engine developer guide covers the API, the CLI and embedding the Engine in another .NET host."},{"u":"technical.html","a":"privacy-and-network","g":"Technical","t":"Privacy and network","h":[["privacy-and-network","Privacy and network"]],"x":"Privacy and network Diagnostics and log analysis run locally, without an AI service or a KillerBench account. Journals and retained evidence stay in the local account's %LOCALAPPDATA%\\KillerBench folders. Actions you select can contact their destination, such as Windows Update, software download services, or recovery-key backup to Active Directory or Entra ID. The startup update check contacts GitHub, and knowledge updates and the KillerMCP installer contact their configured services. An AI client using KillerMCP may receive the reference result it asked for. Raw logs, imported files and exported evidence can contain computer names, accounts, paths or sensitive source text. Review them before sharing, and never include recovery keys, credentials or customer data in a public report."},{"u":"technical.html","a":"development-status","g":"Technical","t":"Development status","h":[["development-status","Development status"]],"x":"Development status KillerBench is in development and there is no public download yet. Repair coverage is still expanding, and native repair validation is still in progress. A successful build or simulated check does not establish native repair behavior. Disposable Windows virtual machines are used to validate each native change across Windows versions and failure states. See How changes stay safe for the full safety model."},{"u":"technical.html","a":"glossary","g":"Technical","t":"Glossary","h":[["glossary","Glossary"]],"x":"Glossary Plain-language definitions of the terms used on this page, in alphabetical order. Term What it means BITS Background Intelligent Transfer Service, the Windows service that downloads updates and other files in the background. Capture A saved .kbench file holding retained evidence, so it can be reviewed again without the original computer. CBS Component-Based Servicing, the Windows subsystem that installs updates and features. Its log is CBS.log . DISM Deployment Image Servicing and Management, the Windows tool that checks and repairs the component store. Elevation Running with administrator rights after a Windows prompt. KillerBench never elevates itself. Evidence What KillerBench actually read from Windows, kept with its source so a finding can be traced back. Fail closed When evidence is ambiguous, missing or too large, the result is Unknown and no change is allowed. Journal A local JSON record written before a change and updated after it, so an interrupted action can be reviewed later. Knowledge pack A versioned, validated file of reference data such as error codes or hardware identifiers. Panther The folder where Windows Setup writes its upgrade logs. Pending guard The record that marks a change as possibly sent to Windows. It blocks a repeat until reconciliation reads the outcome. Policy owner Group Policy or device management that controls a setting. KillerBench leaves owned settings alone. Recipe A typed repair with its own eligibility, execution, verification and recovery rules. Reconciliation Reading fresh state after an interruption to learn what happened, without repeating the change. SFC System File Checker, the Windows tool that verifies and restores protected system files. TPM Trusted Platform Module, the security chip BitLocker uses to protect the drive key. Verified The result was read back from Windows and matched what the change was meant to do. Workflow One tool's run: the checks it collects, the recipes it may use and the report it produces."},{"u":"engine/index.html","g":"Developer guide","t":"The KillerBench.Engine","h":[["what-it-provides","What it provides"],["designed-for-traceability","Designed for traceability"],["diagnostics-first","Diagnostics first"],["desktop-host","Desktop host"],["current-status","Current status"],["choose-a-guide","Choose a guide"]],"x":"The KillerBench.Engine The KillerBench.Engine is a Windows diagnostics and repair library for applications that need structured evidence instead of a collection of opaque scripts. It powers the KillerBench desktop and command-line tools, and it can be embedded in another .NET host. What it provides - Bounded diagnostic workflows for Windows servicing, devices, storage, networking, printing, security, applications, profiles, and other system areas - Typed evidence with explicit complete, incomplete, unknown, and historical states - Repair recipes that require exact current evidence before they can run - Durable journals for repair verification and interrupted-run recovery - Progress callbacks for live displays such as servicing activity - Text, JSON, HTML, and structured .NET results for reports and integrations - Versioned knowledge packs for error codes, hardware identifiers, vendors, and support references Designed for traceability A finding is tied to the evidence that produced it. Historical events stay historical. Missing data does not become a healthy verdict. A failed provider does not become an empty result. The Engine reports the boundary of what it inspected so a host can explain what is known and what remains unknown. Diagnostics first Most Engine operations are read-only. Repair support is deliberately narrower than diagnostic coverage. A repair must have a supported recipe, fresh eligibility evidence, explicit host authorization, a durable preparation record, a bounded native action, and post-action verification. The full system scan is diagnostic-only, including when a focused workflow has automatic repair enabled. Saved captures and historical events never authorize a repair. Native repair validation remains scoped to the retained disposable-Windows-machine evidence recorded in the repository's VM-VALIDATION.md . Desktop host The desktop shell presents Engine output through Raw output and Readable report views. Its confirmations and Open, Save, and Folder dialogs follow the active app theme. Those controls belong to the desktop host; an embedded host must provide its own review and authorization UI while preserving the Engine's evidence, prerequisite, journaling, and verification gates. Current status The Engine targets .NET 10 and Windows. It is in active development and is currently consumed through a project reference inside the KillerBench repository. A public package has not been released. Choose a guide - Getting started shows a first read-only workflow. - Diagnostic workflows explains focused checks and the full system scan. - Evidence and results explains states, journals, progress, and reports. - Repairs and safety describes the evidence gates and recovery model. - CLI guide covers command discovery, text output, and JSON output. - API guide maps the main public entry points. - Architecture explains the Engine boundary and internal layers. - Knowledge packs covers updateable reference data."},{"u":"engine/getting-started.html","g":"Developer guide","t":"Getting started","h":[["requirements","Requirements"],["reference-the-project","Reference the project"],["run-a-focused-workflow","Run a focused workflow"],["run-the-broad-diagnostic-scan","Run the broad diagnostic scan"],["handle-cancellation","Handle cancellation"],["next-steps","Next steps"]],"x":"Getting started Run a read-only diagnostic workflow and inspect its typed result in a few minutes. Requirements - Windows x64 for the current native provider; check Microsoft's .NET 10 Windows requirements (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/install/windows) - .NET 10 SDK - A .NET 10 application - A writable folder for workflow journals The Engine has no WPF dependency. The desktop interface is a separate host. Reference the project Use an authorized source checkout. Reference the Windows provider project from your .NET 10 console host; its project references also include the shared Engine and Engine.Core: Run a focused workflow Call WindowsEngine.Register() once at host startup before resolving native providers. Without registration, the platform-neutral Engine reports that no provider is registered. The example uses implicit .NET usings for System, IO and threading. autoRepair: false keeps the run diagnostic-only. The Engine writes a workflow journal even when no repair is attempted. Run the broad diagnostic scan The full scan combines common diagnostic areas. It does not prove every possible Windows component healthy, and it does not enable every optional repair. Handle cancellation Pass a CancellationToken from your host. The Engine checks cancellation between bounded stages. If a repair may already have reached Windows, the durable journal remains available for reconciliation instead of silently retrying the action. Next steps - Read Diagnostic workflows before selecting workflow kinds. - Read Repairs and safety before exposing any repair action. - Use the CLI guide to explore diagnostics without writing a host application."},{"u":"engine/workflows.html","g":"Developer guide","t":"Diagnostic workflows","h":[["windows-update-medic-service","Windows Update Medic Service"],["focused-workflows","Focused workflows"],["check-states","Check states"],["full-system-scan","Full system scan"],["live-servicing-activity","Live servicing activity"],["progress-and-status","Progress and status"],["workflow-journals","Workflow journals"]],"x":"Diagnostic workflows Workflows collect related evidence and return one WorkflowResult . They are the simplest high-level Engine surface for desktop applications, automation hosts, and support tools. Windows Update Medic Service The focused update-medic-service workflow combines exact current WaaSMedicSvc state, registered ServiceDll presence and signature status, bounded current Windows Update activity and exact Service Control Manager failure history into one conservative conclusion. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. DLL observations, activity and history never authorize a start or component action. A stopped demand-start state remains informational in every broader workflow. When this exact tool is selected, optional recovery can issue one bounded start only after complete dependency, executable registration, administrator, restart and fresh final evidence checks pass. Starting the service can resume Windows remediation or update activity under the current configuration. KillerBench preserves update policy, source enrollment, schedules, packages, BITS jobs, restart settings, service permissions and startup type. Verification covers stable service state only. It does not claim remediation, component health, applicability, download, installation or restart readiness. The focused windows-update-service workflow combines exact current state, registered ServiceDll presence and signature status, bounded current Windows Update activity and exact Service Control Manager failure history into one conservative conclusion. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. DLL observations, activity and history never authorize its separately guarded start. The service can start only from a stopped automatic state after fresh dependency and executable-registration checks. Starting it may resume existing update activity under current policy. Policy, source enrollment, schedules, restart settings, packages, BITS jobs, service permissions and startup type remain unchanged. Stable service state does not establish source reachability, applicability, download, installation, completion or restart readiness. BITS, Cryptographic Services, Windows Modules Installer and Update Orchestrator use their own focused evidence combinations and conservative conclusions. Historical events remain read-only context and cannot authorize a start or component change. The focused delivery-optimization-service workflow shows exact service state, privacy-bounded current transfer aggregates, recognized policy surfaces, exact Service Control Manager failure history and one combined conclusion. Missing evidence takes precedence, while service, policy or historical concerns remain review-only. Historical failures cannot authorize continuation. Running and stopped demand-start states remain informational. Only an exact Paused current state can offer the separate recovery. Execution repeats that state immediately before one bounded continue request and requires two coherent Running observations. Startup type, cache contents, peers, bandwidth, update source and managed policy remain unchanged. Verification does not claim transfer activity, cache health, peer reachability, update progress or completion. The focused bits-service workflow shows current-account transfer jobs, exact current service state, bounded exact Service Control Manager failure history and one combined conclusion before its service recipe is evaluated. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. Historical failures and transfer snapshots cannot authorize a start. Optional recovery still requires exact current stopped-service evidence, dependencies, executable registration, administrator access, clear restart evidence and one unchanged final check. Jobs, priorities, owners, destinations, proxy settings, policy and startup type remain unchanged. Stable service state does not prove transfer progress, application ownership, acknowledgment or completion. The focused cryptographic-service workflow shows exact current service state, registered service DLL presence and signature status, bounded exact Service Control Manager failure history and one combined conclusion. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. Historical failures and DLL observations cannot authorize a service start or component replacement. Optional recovery still requires the normal exact-service prerequisites and one unchanged final check. Certificates, keys, catalogs, trust settings, policy and startup type remain unchanged. Stable service state and a present Valid DLL do not validate certificates, signatures, catalogs, trust chains, update payloads or application behavior. The focused modules-installer-service workflow shows exact current service state, bounded servicing package transitions, exact Service Control Manager failure history and one combined conclusion. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. Historical failures and package states cannot authorize a service start or package operation. Optional recovery still requires the normal exact-service prerequisites and one unchanged final check. Packages, component-store content, sources, policy and startup type remain unchanged. Stable service state does not establish component-store health, package applicability, servicing completion or restart readiness. The focused update-orchestrator-service workflow shows exact current service state, the exact built-in Windows Update Scheduled Start task, bounded current Windows Update activity, exact Service Control Manager failure history and one combined conclusion. Incomplete evidence takes precedence. Task results, activity and historical failures cannot authorize a service start or update operation. Optional recovery still requires the normal exact-service prerequisites and one unchanged final check. Policy, source enrollment, schedules, restart settings, packages, BITS jobs, service permissions and startup type remain unchanged. Stable service state does not establish applicability, download progress, installation progress, "},{"u":"engine/evidence.html","g":"Developer guide","t":"Evidence and results","h":[["provider-evidence","Provider evidence"],["workflow-checks","Workflow checks"],["workflow-results","Workflow results"],["reports","Reports"],["historical-evidence","Historical evidence"],["unknown-is-a-result","Unknown is a result"]],"x":"Evidence and results The Engine separates provider output, interpreted checks, workflow results, and human-readable reports. Each layer keeps uncertainty visible. Provider evidence Native commands and Windows providers run behind fixed time, record, and size bounds. ToolResult records exit status, timeout, truncation, standard output, and error output. A provider failure or truncated result cannot be treated as a clean empty response. Collectors minimize retained data. Many diagnostic results keep names, categories, counts, states, and timestamps while excluding credentials, command lines, full paths, account identities, network endpoints, and unrelated event payloads. Workflow checks WorkflowCheck adds a name, interpreted state, explanation, optional repair kind, and optional provider evidence. Consumers should display the state and detail together. The state alone does not communicate the scope or limitations of the check. Workflow results WorkflowResult contains: - The workflow kind and machine - Start time and final status - Every completed check - Any repair results - Recipe results - The saved journal path Use the typed result for UI decisions. Use reports for people and JSON for interoperable automation. Reports KillerBench can generate diagnostic-only or diagnostic-and-repair reports. The report separates: 1. What was inspected 2. What problem was found 3. What evidence supports the finding 4. What action was proposed or performed 5. How the result was verified 6. What remains unknown or needs a restart HTML reports use the Killer Tools theme system and remain readable without the desktop app. Text reports favor portability. JSON retains structured fields for another program. Historical evidence Event records and imported logs describe what was recorded at a past time. They can identify useful patterns, error codes, and the next diagnostic scope. They do not prove that the fault is still active. Unknown is a result Unknown is not an exception to hide. It tells the host that access was denied, a source was missing, a provider failed, output was malformed, the bound was exceeded, or evidence disagreed. A useful host shows this limitation and offers the relevant next step."},{"u":"engine/repairs.html","g":"Developer guide","t":"Repairs and safety","h":[["repair-sequence","Repair sequence"],["explicit-choices","Explicit choices"],["managed-systems","Managed systems"],["durable-pending-records","Durable pending records"],["automatic-repair","Automatic repair"],["host-responsibility","Host responsibility"]],"x":"Repairs and safety KillerBench repairs are typed operations with eligibility, execution, verification, and recovery rules. They are not arbitrary command strings selected from a log line. Repair sequence 1. Collect complete current evidence. 2. Match one supported recipe. 3. Review required options and consequences. 4. Confirm administrative and policy prerequisites. 5. Recollect the decisive evidence immediately before mutation. 6. Write a durable preparation record. 7. Run one bounded native action. 8. Collect fresh verification evidence. 9. Record a verified, blocked, stopped, or uncertain outcome. If the decisive evidence changes before execution, the Engine stops without performing the action. Explicit choices Option-dependent repairs require exact choices from the host. Examples include a selected volume, printer queue, recovery-key destination, power values, package version, or repair source. The generic automatic runner cannot invent these values. Managed systems Group Policy, device management, and other authority evidence can make a local repair ineffective or inappropriate. Supported coordinators inspect applicable authority state and fail closed when ownership is unknown or managed. Durable pending records A process can stop after Windows receives a request but before verification completes. In that case the Engine retains a pending record. A later reconciliation reads fresh state and determines what happened. Reconciliation never repeats the original mutation automatically. Automatic repair autoRepair means the host allows eligible recipes to proceed. It does not bypass evidence requirements, choose missing options, ignore policy, retry an uncertain action, restart Windows, or turn an Attention finding into a repair. The full system scan remains diagnostic-only even when a focused workflow allows automatic repair. Saved captures, imported evidence, and historical events do not authorize native dispatch. Host responsibility Before enabling a repair, a host should show: - The exact target - The proposed change - Required elevation or restart - Data and settings that remain untouched - The evidence that made the action eligible - The journal and recovery state after execution Use disposable virtual machines to validate native mutations across Windows versions and failure states before exposing them to production users."},{"u":"engine/cli.html","g":"Developer guide","t":"CLI guide","h":[["build","Build"],["discover-commands","Discover commands"],["discover-workflow-controls","Discover workflow controls"],["run-a-workflow","Run a workflow"],["run-focused-diagnostics","Run focused diagnostics"],["live-servicing-monitor","Live servicing monitor"],["review-microsoft-app-removal-package-eligibility","Review Microsoft app removal package eligibility"],["export-the-vm-validation-plan","Export the VM validation plan"],["execution-boundary","Execution boundary"],["exit-behavior","Exit behavior"]],"x":"CLI guide killerbench-cli exposes the Engine for technicians, scripts, test harnesses, and headless collection. Build Discover commands The help output is the authoritative command list for the current checkout. Commands include focused diagnostics, complete workflows, servicing activity, knowledge lookups, planning tools, report generation, and selected guarded repairs. Discover workflow controls The command returns host-neutral JSON for the option-rich workflows. It includes toggles, sliders, choices, text fields, defaults, bounds, dependencies and the execution boundary. Repeated --set KEY=VALUE arguments validate and normalize a proposed option plan, fill omitted defaults and identify an explicit machine-changing request. Validation does not execute the plan, inspect Windows or bypass the separate execution coordinator. Run a workflow Add --json when a command supports structured output: Validated shared controls can run the matching read-only workflow directly: The workflow command accepts repeated --set KEY=VALUE values only after the shared validator accepts the complete plan. It refuses --auto-repair , recipe selection and every option marked as machine-changing. Use the dedicated preview and guarded execution command for a requested repair. The first diagnostic result records the nondefault settings in effect. Sensitive paths, installer switches, protector IDs, package sources and deployment device names are redacted or counted. A prepared System Restore description and reason drive its creation-readiness review. Storage repair review collects exact-volume state plus a bounded read-only filesystem diagnosis, then explains backup, restart or dismount requirements without scheduling CHKDSK. A selected storage optimization method drives the exact-volume analysis. Printing selections correlate one exact queue with current condition and attribute evidence, and one exact job with the bounded visible-job inventory. A selected MSI product is reviewed against its exact registration state, current Installer activity, restart state and administrator access. A selected Windows Time source is reviewed against current role, policy, mode and peer evidence. A selected Event Log channel receives an exact configuration and policy-ownership review. Selected Firewall profiles receive an exact ActiveStore and policy-ownership review. A proposed computer name receives an exact identity, role, restart and administrator readiness review. None of these reviews performs or authorizes the proposed change. Run focused diagnostics Focused commands are useful when a host needs one evidence boundary instead of a full workflow. Examples include: Live servicing monitor The monitor prints completed snapshots as they arrive. It reports recognized activity and evidence, not an invented completion percentage. Review Microsoft app removal package eligibility The plan command repeats current Microsoft app removal inventory and safeguard collection, then evaluates one exact package full name. It is preview-only and connects no removal provider. Preserved-data eligibility is limited to loose-file development packages because Windows does not offer that safeguard for ordinary installed Store/MSIX packages. An eligible result does not remove the package. Export the VM validation plan The command returns schema 2 JSON requirements for legacy workflows, declarative packs and explicit repair commands. A shared recipe ID retains separate implementation identities instead of counting overlapping catalogs as unrelated repairs. Each implementation includes its definition and binary fingerprints, scenario requirements and native evidence status. It runs no diagnostics or repairs. The output is a disposable-VM test plan, not evidence that a native scenario passed. The binary fingerprint covers the exact Engine, Engine.Core and Engine.Windows images. The single-file CLI retains embedded copies of those images so the same shared engine build has the same identity in desktop and CLI hosts. Frontend guards, physical interaction and signed distribution acceptance remain separate release checks. Validate records returned from disposable VMs and summarize current-version coverage: Records must match the recipe version, implementation identity, definition fingerprint, binary fingerprint and scenario ID. They must retain every required artifact with a filename and matching SHA-256 digest. Historical records without exact implementation binding stay stale. Missing artifacts, failed restoration and incomplete scenario coverage cannot establish a verified repair. Coverage keeps Passed, Failed, Blocked, Inconclusive and missing scenarios separate. These commands only read local evidence files and retained artifacts. Generate a readable local coverage report from the same validated records: HTML reports include every installed scenario, status and text filters, and the 13 Killer Tools themes. Missing evidence remains distinct from every recorded outcome. Use --theme , --accent and --overwrite when needed. Report generation does not inspect or change Windows. Execution boundary Most commands are read-only. Commands that can change Windows require their exact execution switch and still enforce the same Engine eligibility, journaling, final-evidence, and verification rules. Unsupported execution is rejected. Exit behavior Treat JSON fields and documented exit behavior as the automation contract. Do not parse decorative console text when structured output is available. Preserve the journal path for later review or reconciliation."},{"u":"engine/api.html","g":"Developer guide","t":"API guide","h":[["high-level-entry-points","High-level entry points"],["core-result-types","Core result types"],["workflow-controls","Workflow controls"],["capability-folders","Capability folders"],["cancellation-and-progress","Cancellation and progress"],["lower-level-collectors","Lower-level collectors"],["versioning","Versioning"]],"x":"API guide Public types use capability namespaces such as KillerBench.Engine.Workflows , KillerBench.Engine.Infrastructure and KillerBench.Engine.Validation . Windows providers use KillerBench.Engine.Windows and its subnamespaces. Reference the provider project and call WindowsEngine.Register() before running native workflows; see Getting started . High-level entry points Task Start with --- --- Run one diagnostic area Workflows.RunAsync and WorkflowKind Run common diagnostics FullSystemScan.RunAsync Show available common areas CommonDiagnosticCatalog.All Show repair recipes RecipeCatalog.ForWorkflow Build workflow controls WorkflowOptionCatalog.All or WorkflowOptionCatalog.ForWorkflow Observe servicing activity ServicingActivityMonitor.ObserveAsync Interpret a completed workflow WorkflowResult and WorkflowRecoveryPlan.From Review a saved journal WorkflowJournalReview Generate reports Engine report and export types Core result types Type Purpose --- --- WorkflowCheck One interpreted diagnostic result with state, detail, evidence, and optional repair WorkflowResult Complete workflow output plus journal location ToolResult Bounded native-provider result including timeout and truncation state RepairRecipe Eligibility and execution metadata for one supported repair RecipeResult Outcome of evaluating or executing a recipe RepairResult Native repair steps, verification, and durable outcome WorkflowOptionSet Host-neutral controls, defaults and execution boundary for one option-rich workflow WorkflowOptionDefinition A toggle, slider, choice or text control with validation metadata and dependencies WorkflowOptionValidationResult Normalized values, validation errors and whether an explicit machine-changing control was requested WorkflowOptionProjection Converts a valid read-only option plan into Engine workflow or servicing-monitor options Workflow controls WorkflowOptionCatalog lets a desktop, CLI or embedded host discover the same supported controls without copying UI definitions. Each option has a stable key, label, control type, default value and explanation. Sliders include their minimum, maximum and step. Choices expose exact values. Conditional controls identify the option and value that enables them. ChangesMachine is true only for an implemented explicit execution control. These controls are advanced and off by default. The flag describes the control surface; it does not bypass fresh evidence, eligibility, journaling, verification or recovery requirements. Pass submitted key/value pairs to WorkflowOptionValidation.Validate . The validator fills omitted defaults, normalizes booleans and integers, and rejects unknown or duplicate keys, invalid choices, out-of-range or off-step sliders, oversized text, missing required values and conditional controls that are not active. A valid machine-changing request still authorizes nothing by itself. The operation-specific coordinator must collect fresh evidence and enforce its own execution boundary. After validation, WorkflowOptionProjection.CreateHost projects safe values into a WindowsWorkflowHost . It also adds a redacted Selected workflow options result so the normalized nondefault plan remains visible in reports and journals. Prepared System Restore settings drive the creation-readiness review. Selected filesystem repair settings drive exact-volume state collection, bounded read-only diagnosis and consequence review without authorizing or scheduling repair. Selected storage optimization settings drive exact-volume analysis. Selected printing targets correlate one exact queue and optional job with current bounded evidence without authorizing a queue or job change. A selected MSI product receives one exact repair review that combines current registration, Installer activity, restart state and administrator access while leaving execution with the guarded MSI coordinator. A selected Windows Time source is reviewed against the current role, policy ownership, local mode and peer configuration. A selected Event Log channel is reviewed against exact current configuration and policy ownership. Selected Firewall profiles are reviewed against complete ActiveStore state and policy ownership. A proposed computer name is reviewed against active and configured identity, role, restart state and administrator access. These reviews expose no repair identity. CreateServicingOptions projects the servicing monitor controls into ServicingMonitorOptions . Both reject invalid plans and every plan that requests a machine change. This gives CLI and embedded hosts one shared read-only execution path while leaving repair authorization with the operation-specific coordinators. The shared catalog currently includes desktop controls for Network, Audio, System Restore, Storage, Printing, Windows Installer, Windows Time, Event Log, Profiles, Computer Name, Background Transfers, App Packages, Installed Applications, Search, Component Cleanup, Windows Recovery, Firewall Services and BitLocker. Printing distinguishes queue repair, default selection and one blocked-job cancellation. Installer accepts only an optional normalized MSI product code. Time validates existing, domain and custom-peer choices. Event Log limits enablement to the four inspected diagnostic channels. Profile controls define the inactivity threshold and optional dependency review without authorizing profile changes. Computer Name requires one valid exact proposed name and keeps rename execution explicit. Background Transfers exposes running-account and administrator-only all-users inventory scopes without authorizing a job change. Package and application controls expose only the scopes implemented by their read-only collectors. Search rebuild, component cleanup and Windows Recovery enablement remain off by default and require their existing guarded coordinators. Firewall execution requires one or more exact profile selections. BitLocker validates mutually exclusive backup, encryption and protection-resume actions,"},{"u":"engine/architecture.html","g":"Developer guide","t":"Architecture","h":[["boundary","Boundary"],["project-relationships","Project relationships"],["internal-layers","Internal layers"],["windows-and-shell-modes","Windows and shell modes"],["failure-model","Failure model"]],"x":"Architecture The Engine is split into UI-free Engine.Core, Engine and Engine.Windows projects. Desktop, CLI and tests are separate hosts. Engine.Core supplies primitives and declarative recipes, Engine owns shared workflows and evidence, and Engine.Windows registers the native providers. Boundary The Engine owns: - Bounded Windows evidence collection - Deterministic interpretation and correlation - Workflow and recipe catalogs - Repair eligibility and execution coordination - Durable journals and no-repeat reconciliation - Report models and knowledge-pack readers The host owns: - Navigation, dialogs, controls, and presentation - User choices and consent - UAC relaunch behavior - Progress and cancellation controls - File pickers and report destinations - Product updates and deployment Project relationships The Engine does not reference WPF or desktop controls. Tests can substitute provider and repair hosts so parsers, eligibility, safety gates, interruption behavior, and recovery can be verified without changing the development computer. Internal layers Layer Responsibility --- --- Capability collectors Read bounded Windows state for one exact scope Evidence parsers Validate shape, identity, completeness, and limits Diagnostic interpretation Convert evidence into explicit workflow states Workflow orchestration Combine checks, progress, recipes, and journals Repair coordinators Recheck eligibility, prepare, mutate, verify, and reconcile Reports Present problems, actions, results, and limitations Knowledge packs Resolve versioned external reference data without changing logic Windows and shell modes The current native provider layer targets Windows and uses .NET, Windows APIs, PowerShell, and Windows command-line tools where appropriate. The Engine is not cross-platform today. A future Bash provider would be a separate host implementation behind explicit interfaces. It would need its own capability catalog, evidence contracts, safety rules, and tests. Translating PowerShell command strings into Bash would not create equivalent diagnostics. Failure model Collectors fail closed when identity is ambiguous, output is malformed, a limit is exceeded, or required authority cannot be inspected. Repair coordinators separate preview, preparation, dispatch, verification, and reconciliation so an interrupted process does not automatically repeat a machine change."},{"u":"engine/knowledge-packs.html","g":"Developer guide","t":"Knowledge packs","h":[["suitable-data","Suitable data"],["pack-contract","Pack contract"],["generators","Generators"],["updates","Updates"],["separation-from-diagnostics","Separation from diagnostics"]],"x":"Knowledge packs Knowledge packs keep large and frequently updated reference data outside the Engine's diagnostic logic. Suitable data - Windows and Setup error codes - Vendor and device identifiers - PCI, USB, ACPI, and UEFI references - Network OUI vendor data - Driver and support locations - Application and package metadata - Curated support links and diagnostic scopes Pack contract A pack should identify its format, schema version, source, source revision, generation time, and content hash. The Engine validates those fields before using the data. Unknown or invalid packs remain unavailable rather than silently falling back to guesses. Generators Generators belong in the repository when they are required to reproduce a distributed pack. They should: 1. Download only from named authoritative sources. 2. Record source revisions and licenses. 3. Validate input before transformation. 4. Normalize data deterministically. 5. Write a manifest and content hash. 6. Produce the same output from the same source input. Large generated databases and temporary downloads do not belong in the Engine source folder. The repository can contain small fixtures for parser and schema tests. Updates The desktop About surface can offer an Update databases action. An update should download to a temporary location, validate the manifest and hash, then replace the active pack atomically. A failed update must leave the prior valid pack available. Separation from diagnostics Knowledge data can explain an identifier or suggest a next diagnostic scope. It cannot convert a historical code into a current fault, choose a driver automatically, or authorize a repair. Current machine evidence remains the deciding input."}];
